WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.660
So, welcome everyone.
Thanks for coming.

00:00:07.661 --> 00:00:10.050
So, today I'm
extremely excited to

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:13.595
be introducing Cynthia
Breazeal from MIT,

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:15.180
where she's the director
of the personal

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.215
robots group at the Media Lab.

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:19.200
Cynthia is one of

00:00:19.201 --> 00:00:21.930
the pioneers of the field
of human robot interaction,

00:00:21.931 --> 00:00:24.760
and she literally wrote
the book on social robotics.

00:00:24.761 --> 00:00:26.640
Cynthia is also
the founder and Chief

00:00:26.641 --> 00:00:29.000
Experience Officer of Geebo,

00:00:29.001 --> 00:00:30.960
really the first social robot for

00:00:30.961 --> 00:00:34.605
the home that is an actual
working consumer robot,

00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:37.660
and it's a real success story
for moving HRI out of

00:00:37.661 --> 00:00:41.275
the lab and into the real world
with real people.

00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:43.420
Geebo even made the cover of

00:00:43.421 --> 00:00:45.370
Time Magazine's 2017 issue on

00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:47.990
the 25 best inventions
of the year.

00:00:47.991 --> 00:00:50.110
She has a long long list of

00:00:50.111 --> 00:00:52.900
other awards and achievements
over the years with

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:55.449
recognition by the National
Academy of Engineering

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.855
as well as the national
design awards,

00:00:57.856 --> 00:01:00.540
but rather than list a whole
bunch more of these awards,

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:02.190
I'll go ahead and hand
it over to Cynthia.

00:01:02.191 --> 00:01:05.780
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.380
All right, well it's
a pleasure to be here.

00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:11.840
I've had such a great visit,

00:01:11.841 --> 00:01:14.060
seeing old friends and meeting
new friends and talking

00:01:14.061 --> 00:01:17.619
about living with AI and robots.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.055
So, I started this
work in social robots,

00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:24.060
I won't tell you how long
ago, it was a long time ago.

00:01:24.061 --> 00:01:28.515
But the thing that is so
striking to me right now is just

00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:30.770
who is living and interacting

00:01:30.771 --> 00:01:33.320
with AI today as part
of our daily lives,

00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:36.100
I mean even fairly recently with

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:40.570
2014 when Alexa was
announced in December.

00:01:40.571 --> 00:01:42.320
There's like 43 million of

00:01:42.321 --> 00:01:44.540
these smart speakers
in people's homes now,

00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:46.685
and with that again
a profound shift

00:01:46.686 --> 00:01:49.190
in who is interacting
with AI and just

00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:52.940
acknowledging there is
so much opportunity

00:01:52.941 --> 00:01:54.200
and so much we still

00:01:54.201 --> 00:01:56.630
have yet to understand about what

00:01:56.631 --> 00:01:59.180
it is to live with
intelligent machines,

00:01:59.181 --> 00:02:01.130
when you think about
the diversity of users,

00:02:01.131 --> 00:02:02.570
from young children to

00:02:02.571 --> 00:02:05.615
our oldest seniors and
everyone in between.

00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:10.295
I think quite a lot about
the home in particular.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.170
I'm sure you've all heard

00:02:12.171 --> 00:02:15.650
the media clips of people
like oh the future

00:02:15.651 --> 00:02:17.810
is going to be like
the Starship Enterprise and you

00:02:17.811 --> 00:02:20.490
have this you know
this embodied voice,

00:02:20.491 --> 00:02:22.080
computer, this computer that,

00:02:22.081 --> 00:02:23.540
and for me, it's like,

00:02:23.541 --> 00:02:25.560
the home is a special place.

00:02:25.561 --> 00:02:27.515
I don't want it to
feel like work.

00:02:27.516 --> 00:02:30.145
I don't want it to feel
like I'm on the subway.

00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:32.710
It requires, I think
a different kind

00:02:32.711 --> 00:02:35.110
of ways of thinking
about the design

00:02:35.111 --> 00:02:38.110
and how you fit an AI
technology into

00:02:38.111 --> 00:02:39.280
the place that is in

00:02:39.281 --> 00:02:41.560
so many ways your most
intimate important place,

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.035
it's a place where
people who love live,

00:02:44.036 --> 00:02:46.810
that really makes me also

00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:49.555
think quite a lot about
our future with living with

00:02:49.556 --> 00:02:52.030
these AI systems and what
this is going to mean

00:02:52.031 --> 00:02:55.355
in these very important
spaces like the home.

00:02:55.356 --> 00:02:59.500
We know it's not fraught with
without important issues.

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:01.900
So this news article
was very striking

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:04.350
to me when it came out 2017.

00:03:04.351 --> 00:03:05.800
So, as you may recall,

00:03:05.801 --> 00:03:08.530
Mattel was going to
basically create an Alexa

00:03:08.531 --> 00:03:11.350
like talking speaker
for the home really

00:03:11.351 --> 00:03:13.390
catering towards
not only young parents

00:03:13.391 --> 00:03:15.130
being able to reorder
diapers and things like

00:03:15.131 --> 00:03:18.130
that but interacting
with kids directly

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:20.440
and it went very

00:03:20.441 --> 00:03:22.600
far down the product
development pipeline.

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:26.140
It was about to be released
for Christmas and they pulled

00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:27.970
the product because there were

00:03:27.971 --> 00:03:30.430
just a lot of concerns expressed,

00:03:30.431 --> 00:03:32.540
not because of things that they

00:03:32.541 --> 00:03:34.770
knew were bad about
this technology,

00:03:34.771 --> 00:03:37.150
but just we didn't know, right?

00:03:37.151 --> 00:03:40.190
We didn't know. So,
child advocacy groups,

00:03:40.191 --> 00:03:42.890
concerns around privacy,
lawmakers, parents.

00:03:42.891 --> 00:03:45.905
Again which is kind of
speaks to the time right so,

00:03:45.906 --> 00:03:48.590
we just have to be very
mindful about bringing

00:03:48.591 --> 00:03:50.000
these technologies into home and

00:03:50.001 --> 00:03:52.295
appreciating that as
researchers and scientists.

00:03:52.296 --> 00:03:54.470
There's so much more to

00:03:54.471 --> 00:03:57.180
creating these technologies
that we have to be aware of.

00:03:57.181 --> 00:03:58.950
So, today we have

00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:01.040
these smart speakers I
would say in general,

00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:02.600
a lot of AI is designed to

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:05.450
be very transactional in nature.

00:04:05.451 --> 00:04:06.845
It's very much like

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:10.595
an intelligent tool to
help you make decisions,

00:04:10.596 --> 00:04:12.585
but that's quite different from

00:04:12.586 --> 00:04:15.075
kind of the vision
that I grew up with.

00:04:15.076 --> 00:04:17.670
Growing up with Star Wars
and thinking about

00:04:17.671 --> 00:04:21.060
these intelligent machines
not just as digital systems,

00:04:21.061 --> 00:04:22.890
but really as these kind
of sidekicks and

00:04:22.891 --> 00:04:28.170
these helpful companion
that weren't just useful,

00:04:28.171 --> 00:04:31.295
but really connected to
us on this human level,

00:04:31.296 --> 00:04:33.000
and they not only helped us

00:04:33.001 --> 00:04:34.770
do useful things
that matter to us,

00:04:34.771 --> 00:04:36.210
they actually even
helped us become

00:04:36.211 --> 00:04:38.205
the people who we
aspire to be, right?

00:04:38.206 --> 00:04:40.070
And that was really the vision

00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:41.930
that I've grown up with
of what this technology

00:04:41.931 --> 00:04:44.630
can mean to us and
the exciting thing is now

00:04:44.631 --> 00:04:46.580
these technologies are starting

00:04:46.581 --> 00:04:48.190
to come into
real world environments.

00:04:48.191 --> 00:04:49.780
We brought them into schools,

00:04:49.781 --> 00:04:51.395
looking at robots as

00:04:51.396 --> 00:04:53.640
language learning companions
into hospital,

00:04:53.641 --> 00:04:56.030
pediatrics to work with
Child Life Specialists to

00:04:56.031 --> 00:04:57.410
assisted living
facilities and now of

00:04:57.411 --> 00:04:59.280
course they're finally
coming into the home.

00:04:59.281 --> 00:05:03.030
So, again what's the evolution
of AI in the home?

00:05:03.031 --> 00:05:05.440
Is it going to be from this sort
of transactional AI that

00:05:05.441 --> 00:05:07.880
we have right now and
digital assistants?

00:05:07.881 --> 00:05:10.160
Will it become the sort
of different vision of

00:05:10.161 --> 00:05:11.360
this helpful companion that's

00:05:11.361 --> 00:05:14.465
a much more kind of
humanistic design?

00:05:14.466 --> 00:05:17.160
I think the crux of
the matter is again,

00:05:17.161 --> 00:05:19.440
especially when you
talk about robots now,

00:05:19.441 --> 00:05:20.910
we still don't
understand very much

00:05:20.911 --> 00:05:22.390
about this long-term
interaction, right?

00:05:22.391 --> 00:05:25.200
When we talk about
a research setting,

00:05:25.201 --> 00:05:28.140
it is hard expensive
logistically challenging

00:05:28.141 --> 00:05:31.590
work to deploy these systems
in the real world,

00:05:31.591 --> 00:05:32.700
into real environments where

00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:34.295
they're interacting
with real people,

00:05:34.296 --> 00:05:36.000
where you're innovating
on the algorithms

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:37.410
and the capabilities
as well as trying to

00:05:37.411 --> 00:05:39.360
understand what's the impact or

00:05:39.361 --> 00:05:42.340
benefit of these technologies
on real people's lives.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:43.420
So, this is just a paper.

00:05:43.421 --> 00:05:45.630
It was written in 2013,

00:05:45.631 --> 00:05:48.035
we actually did an updated
version of this looking at

00:05:48.036 --> 00:05:50.060
all the longitudinal human-robot

00:05:50.061 --> 00:05:51.980
interaction studies ever done,

00:05:51.981 --> 00:05:53.285
many of them were basically

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.070
not even innovating
on the algorithm's,

00:05:55.071 --> 00:05:57.920
just taking a commercial
product like Clio or

00:05:57.921 --> 00:05:59.840
whatnot into
the environment and just

00:05:59.841 --> 00:06:02.000
looking to see what people
did over time,

00:06:02.001 --> 00:06:03.530
and the punchline is there's just

00:06:03.531 --> 00:06:05.220
not a whole lot of
studies out there.

00:06:05.221 --> 00:06:09.635
So, there was a real need
for this kind of work,

00:06:09.636 --> 00:06:12.080
and there's a real need
I think for coupling

00:06:12.081 --> 00:06:15.075
this with the
algorithmic innovation.

00:06:15.076 --> 00:06:16.865
We need better tools,

00:06:16.866 --> 00:06:19.835
we need better processes
to really help

00:06:19.836 --> 00:06:24.205
galvanize and empower the field
to advance this forward.

00:06:24.206 --> 00:06:27.875
When I think about AI in
the big picture, right?

00:06:27.876 --> 00:06:29.320
Again there's a lot of discussion

00:06:29.321 --> 00:06:33.000
around productivity
and efficiency,

00:06:33.001 --> 00:06:35.030
the future of work,

00:06:35.031 --> 00:06:39.370
but I feel that the promise
of AI will not achieve

00:06:39.371 --> 00:06:41.500
its full potential
if it can't address

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:43.890
these very deep human needs.

00:06:43.891 --> 00:06:46.280
So I asked a question
in a lot of my work,

00:06:46.281 --> 00:06:48.450
can AI actually help
us to flourish?

00:06:48.451 --> 00:06:50.500
Not just to be more
productive but to

00:06:50.501 --> 00:06:53.325
live better quality lives
and to be better people?

00:06:53.326 --> 00:06:56.710
When you look at kind of
operationalize frameworks

00:06:56.711 --> 00:06:58.470
around things like well-being,

00:06:58.471 --> 00:07:00.290
it's very clear that it goes

00:07:00.291 --> 00:07:02.070
way beyond the cognitive, right?

00:07:02.071 --> 00:07:03.790
You have to think
about the social,

00:07:03.791 --> 00:07:07.030
the emotional, the sense of
achievement and meaning.

00:07:07.031 --> 00:07:08.980
If we're going to
create AIs that help

00:07:08.981 --> 00:07:11.599
us to live better quality lives,

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.460
we have to create AIs that can

00:07:13.461 --> 00:07:15.550
engage and support us on
all of these dimensions,

00:07:15.551 --> 00:07:16.780
and I would say although there's

00:07:16.781 --> 00:07:18.535
a lot of work on the cognitive,

00:07:18.536 --> 00:07:22.870
the social emotional is
relatively very understudied.

00:07:22.871 --> 00:07:25.570
What we are starting
to find out is as you

00:07:25.571 --> 00:07:28.590
create these social robots,

00:07:28.591 --> 00:07:30.430
it's not as if they're like

00:07:30.431 --> 00:07:31.990
like people who you're
going to replace

00:07:31.991 --> 00:07:35.415
people or like other kinds
of tools like smart devices.

00:07:35.416 --> 00:07:37.540
They're kind of
this new amalgam of

00:07:37.541 --> 00:07:39.580
relationships that we
kind of understand,

00:07:39.581 --> 00:07:41.695
but in this new kind of
intersection, right?

00:07:41.696 --> 00:07:44.290
So there are attributtes
of these systems that

00:07:44.291 --> 00:07:47.080
engage you like a motivating
collaborative ally,

00:07:47.081 --> 00:07:48.880
and I think that's
really important.

00:07:48.881 --> 00:07:51.850
There are aspects of course
of a robot that can be

00:07:51.851 --> 00:07:53.710
an internet cloud
connected tool like

00:07:53.711 --> 00:07:55.120
any other kind of
computing device

00:07:55.121 --> 00:07:56.545
that's kind of like a given.

00:07:56.546 --> 00:07:59.440
But the other thing that's
fascinating is the way people

00:07:59.441 --> 00:08:04.240
naturally seem to want to
relate to it as an other,

00:08:04.241 --> 00:08:05.710
where there's
these attributes of being

00:08:05.711 --> 00:08:07.570
like an attentive
companion animal.

00:08:07.571 --> 00:08:09.640
This sort of non-judgmental

00:08:09.641 --> 00:08:11.410
affectionate and I think you can

00:08:11.411 --> 00:08:13.620
appreciate in that video
I just showed you.

00:08:13.621 --> 00:08:18.040
The nature of how people
interact with this technology is

00:08:18.041 --> 00:08:20.425
very different than a tablet

00:08:20.426 --> 00:08:22.925
or a computer or a VR headset.

00:08:22.926 --> 00:08:25.000
It is this social emotional

00:08:25.001 --> 00:08:27.990
relational connected
other experience,

00:08:27.991 --> 00:08:29.470
and that actually can be very

00:08:29.471 --> 00:08:32.965
empowering for people if
designed in the right way.

00:08:32.966 --> 00:08:38.570
So, in the talk I'm
going to highlight this.

00:08:38.571 --> 00:08:40.050
I think kind of
the punchline of a lot

00:08:40.051 --> 00:08:41.520
of the work that I've
done so whether we're

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:43.170
talking about a social robot or I

00:08:43.171 --> 00:08:46.200
think any other kind of
technology that's trying to

00:08:46.201 --> 00:08:49.410
support human flourishing
is the more you

00:08:49.411 --> 00:08:53.130
can support people on
all of these dimensions,

00:08:53.131 --> 00:08:55.110
we as human beings
are clearly not

00:08:55.111 --> 00:08:56.800
purely cognitive
creatures, right?

00:08:56.801 --> 00:08:58.290
We're deeply social, deeply

00:08:58.291 --> 00:09:00.170
emotional deeply
physical creatures.

00:09:00.171 --> 00:09:03.790
All of these things
influence how we learn,

00:09:03.791 --> 00:09:04.890
how we make decisions,

00:09:04.891 --> 00:09:06.990
how we perceive
the world around us,

00:09:06.991 --> 00:09:09.400
how we're influenced
by events around us.

00:09:09.401 --> 00:09:11.420
But the more you can
support and engage

00:09:11.421 --> 00:09:13.970
people in this holistic way,

00:09:13.971 --> 00:09:15.785
what we find is the more deeply

00:09:15.786 --> 00:09:17.790
people will invest
in that interaction,

00:09:17.791 --> 00:09:20.670
and when people
invest more deeply,

00:09:20.671 --> 00:09:21.860
they can often be more

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:23.780
successful and I think
that's the punchline,

00:09:23.781 --> 00:09:25.490
is they can be even
more successful with

00:09:25.491 --> 00:09:27.050
the technology than if you

00:09:27.051 --> 00:09:29.500
don't design to support
this holistic experience.

00:09:29.501 --> 00:09:32.005
So, I want to talk about,

00:09:32.006 --> 00:09:34.820
this is kind of touching
a little mix of research and

00:09:34.821 --> 00:09:37.280
application of what is a

00:09:37.281 --> 00:09:43.515
different about this experience
with the social robot?

00:09:43.516 --> 00:09:45.840
A lot of times,

00:09:45.841 --> 00:09:47.135
we get asked the question,

00:09:47.136 --> 00:09:50.470
still today, does it matter
that it's physically embody?

00:09:50.471 --> 00:09:52.140
Does it matter that it's a robot?

00:09:52.141 --> 00:09:56.435
Ironically, I would say when
we first started this field,

00:09:56.436 --> 00:09:59.170
we would get papers
just rejected from

00:09:59.171 --> 00:10:01.635
Chi Point Blank just
because it was on a robot,

00:10:01.636 --> 00:10:03.980
and reviewers would say things
like, "Why is it a robot?

00:10:03.981 --> 00:10:06.725
It's so expensive, why not
just make it a virtual agent?

00:10:06.726 --> 00:10:08.000
So, the thing that we had to

00:10:08.001 --> 00:10:09.380
compete with against
was basically

00:10:09.381 --> 00:10:12.890
a screen with a virtual avatar.

00:10:12.891 --> 00:10:14.675
Now, we're having defend

00:10:14.676 --> 00:10:16.220
ourselves against smart speakers,

00:10:16.221 --> 00:10:19.840
and that seems like a huge
several steps of backwards,

00:10:19.841 --> 00:10:21.920
you're taking even more
[inaudible] stimulus,

00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:24.735
and you're asking us to validate
ourselves base on that.

00:10:24.736 --> 00:10:26.460
So, again, I guess that's
just the lesson and

00:10:26.461 --> 00:10:28.645
kind of combating
ubiquity, right?

00:10:28.646 --> 00:10:30.260
Once a ubiquitous
then suddenly you

00:10:30.261 --> 00:10:31.900
don't have to fight
these battles anymore.

00:10:31.901 --> 00:10:33.480
But we get asked
this question a lot.

00:10:33.481 --> 00:10:35.220
So, what is it about the

00:10:35.221 --> 00:10:37.430
social so co-presence? Is
that actually really matter?

00:10:37.431 --> 00:10:39.695
So, I want to present
work as a kind of

00:10:39.696 --> 00:10:43.430
a case study in work that we've
been doing in pediatrics.

00:10:43.431 --> 00:10:45.825
This is a long-standing
collaboration

00:10:45.826 --> 00:10:47.590
with Boston Children's Hospital,

00:10:47.591 --> 00:10:50.115
where we've been working
with Child Life Specialists,

00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:51.580
and Child Life Specialists

00:10:51.581 --> 00:10:53.910
are professionals who
are trained to address

00:10:53.911 --> 00:10:55.810
the emotional support and

00:10:55.811 --> 00:10:58.180
needs of children patients
who are in the hospital,

00:10:58.181 --> 00:11:00.095
but also that of their families.

00:11:00.096 --> 00:11:01.780
The question of course is,

00:11:01.781 --> 00:11:03.879
not surprising a lot
of these domains,

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:07.460
there are not enough Child
Life Specialists to meet

00:11:07.461 --> 00:11:12.140
the demands of the patient,
admitted patients.

00:11:12.141 --> 00:11:14.330
So, the question of
how can you create

00:11:14.331 --> 00:11:17.040
a different kind of
technology that is

00:11:17.041 --> 00:11:18.270
an extension of the Child

00:11:18.271 --> 00:11:19.830
Life Services team
that helps them

00:11:19.831 --> 00:11:22.850
basically do their job in
a more scalable effective way,

00:11:22.851 --> 00:11:24.400
is actually really,
really interesting.

00:11:24.401 --> 00:11:27.645
I would also say
another side effect

00:11:27.646 --> 00:11:29.160
that is also how can you

00:11:29.161 --> 00:11:30.980
extend that quality
of care to the home?

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:32.325
Because you can maybe provide

00:11:32.326 --> 00:11:34.515
exceptional quality of
care in the hospital,

00:11:34.516 --> 00:11:36.450
but they need to be
able to continue engage

00:11:36.451 --> 00:11:38.540
patients at home and they
have no way of doing that.

00:11:38.541 --> 00:11:40.465
So, again the notion of a robot,

00:11:40.466 --> 00:11:42.440
a different kind of
technology to do that,

00:11:42.441 --> 00:11:44.190
is really, really
interesting to them.

00:11:44.191 --> 00:11:45.660
So, this is just a video

00:11:45.661 --> 00:11:47.040
that was done by
The New York Times,

00:11:47.041 --> 00:11:48.490
kind of giving you a little bit

00:11:48.491 --> 00:11:50.945
of color on what this looks like.

00:11:50.946 --> 00:11:52.990
&gt;&gt; For patients like Beatrice

00:11:52.991 --> 00:11:54.475
who's got a chronic condition.

00:11:54.476 --> 00:11:56.275
She's going to be in and
out of the hospital.

00:11:56.276 --> 00:11:57.920
She's here for procedures.

00:11:57.921 --> 00:12:00.740
She's here for doctor's visits.
She's missing school.

00:12:00.741 --> 00:12:01.830
She's missing her friends.

00:12:01.831 --> 00:12:03.660
She's really not doing
any of the things

00:12:03.661 --> 00:12:05.755
that a normal kid
her age is doing.

00:12:05.756 --> 00:12:07.495
What we want to offer
kids like that it's

00:12:07.496 --> 00:12:09.225
just one more way of helping

00:12:09.226 --> 00:12:11.185
them to feel okay where they are

00:12:11.186 --> 00:12:13.925
and what is otherwise
a really stressful experience.

00:12:13.926 --> 00:12:17.510
I think there's a way of
connecting with kids,

00:12:17.511 --> 00:12:20.640
that's different from
what grown ups can offer.

00:12:20.641 --> 00:12:23.215
They have incredible
imaginations.

00:12:23.216 --> 00:12:26.440
They can really suspend
disbelief and there can be

00:12:26.441 --> 00:12:27.845
a true relationship that develops

00:12:27.846 --> 00:12:30.580
between Huggable and patient.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:39.370
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:12:39.371 --> 00:12:39.520
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:12:39.521 --> 00:12:40.585
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:12:40.586 --> 00:12:41.710
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:12:41.711 --> 00:12:41.810
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:12:41.811 --> 00:12:52.340
&gt;&gt; Here you go. Is that better?

00:12:54.345 --> 00:12:56.170
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:12:56.171 --> 00:12:58.220
&gt;&gt; Hi. It's Huggable.

00:12:58.221 --> 00:13:00.460
&gt;&gt; Hi Huggable.

00:13:00.461 --> 00:13:01.840
&gt;&gt; Hi Beatrice.

00:13:01.841 --> 00:13:05.745
&gt;&gt; You're adorable.

00:13:05.746 --> 00:13:07.375
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:13:07.376 --> 00:13:10.615
&gt;&gt; It's very nice to meet you.

00:13:10.616 --> 00:13:12.790
Do you want to play a game?

00:13:12.791 --> 00:13:15.485
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Let's play a game.

00:13:15.486 --> 00:13:18.300
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:13:18.301 --> 00:13:19.990
So what we have here
in this case is

00:13:19.991 --> 00:13:21.700
that we just finished
a clinical trial.

00:13:21.701 --> 00:13:24.440
We have a Child Life
Specialist who works

00:13:24.441 --> 00:13:27.540
in the room with one of
three interventions,

00:13:27.541 --> 00:13:29.185
so we compared the physical robot

00:13:29.186 --> 00:13:32.150
versus a plug compatible,

00:13:32.151 --> 00:13:34.160
graphical version of
a robot on tablet

00:13:34.161 --> 00:13:36.455
versus standard of
care is a plush.

00:13:36.456 --> 00:13:38.560
The social agents is

00:13:38.561 --> 00:13:40.890
tele-operated by
another Child Life Specialists.

00:13:40.891 --> 00:13:42.510
There's a lot of
reasons why we do that.

00:13:42.511 --> 00:13:43.850
One is just because
we want to make

00:13:43.851 --> 00:13:45.745
sure that the quality
of care is present.

00:13:45.746 --> 00:13:47.800
We also want to collect
a lot of data to

00:13:47.801 --> 00:13:50.330
understand what is
the phenomenon of having

00:13:50.331 --> 00:13:52.840
a robot in this context

00:13:52.841 --> 00:13:55.540
that we can then think
about what are the AIs,

00:13:55.541 --> 00:13:56.890
what are the opportunities

00:13:56.891 --> 00:13:58.560
to create more autonomy for this.

00:13:58.561 --> 00:14:01.050
So, we often start
projects with this sort of

00:14:01.051 --> 00:14:03.540
tele-operation paradigm
to begin with.

00:14:03.541 --> 00:14:05.065
We get a lot of
insights from that.

00:14:05.066 --> 00:14:07.880
So, we just finished
a clinical trial comparing

00:14:07.881 --> 00:14:09.555
these three interventions
which I think

00:14:09.556 --> 00:14:11.330
one of the things that's
nice about this study,

00:14:11.331 --> 00:14:14.060
is the Child Life Specialist
in the room is doing

00:14:14.061 --> 00:14:15.260
her job to the best of

00:14:15.261 --> 00:14:17.070
her ability with each
of these interventions.

00:14:17.071 --> 00:14:18.860
So, in many ways, all
three of them are

00:14:18.861 --> 00:14:20.945
socially animated whether that's

00:14:20.946 --> 00:14:22.490
the remote Child Life Specialist

00:14:22.491 --> 00:14:25.280
tele-operating the virtual bear
or the physical bear,

00:14:25.281 --> 00:14:27.450
or if it's if the Child
Life Specialist kind

00:14:27.451 --> 00:14:29.290
of puppeteering the plush
with the child, right.

00:14:29.291 --> 00:14:34.255
So all three of these are
animate one way or another.

00:14:34.256 --> 00:14:36.730
We looked at the
difference between

00:14:36.731 --> 00:14:38.580
these technologies
across a lot of

00:14:38.581 --> 00:14:40.565
different dimensions where
we were particularly

00:14:40.566 --> 00:14:43.045
interested in the social
emotional factors.

00:14:43.046 --> 00:14:44.410
If you're going to try to create

00:14:44.411 --> 00:14:45.980
a technology
intervention that can

00:14:45.981 --> 00:14:50.635
engage children and improve
their emotional experience.

00:14:50.636 --> 00:14:52.210
We are very interested
in looking at that,

00:14:52.211 --> 00:14:53.790
where we should be
looking at engagement

00:14:53.791 --> 00:14:55.680
how often children
would speak because if

00:14:55.681 --> 00:14:57.140
you want to engage a trial in

00:14:57.141 --> 00:14:59.410
a health protocol
in the hospital,

00:14:59.411 --> 00:15:02.235
their engagement cooperativeness
is very important.

00:15:02.236 --> 00:15:05.630
If you look at
these different charts,

00:15:05.631 --> 00:15:10.590
what we see is for things
like total joyful,

00:15:10.591 --> 00:15:13.465
a semantic effective analysis,

00:15:13.466 --> 00:15:15.270
sentiment analysis
on the utterances

00:15:15.271 --> 00:15:16.865
that the children
would speak over time,

00:15:16.866 --> 00:15:18.425
we see that the positive effect

00:15:18.426 --> 00:15:20.810
increases over time
with a physical robot,

00:15:20.811 --> 00:15:22.720
but it even decreases
over time with

00:15:22.721 --> 00:15:25.725
the virtual robot and the plush
is kind of the lowest.

00:15:25.726 --> 00:15:27.940
When we look at shared attention,

00:15:27.941 --> 00:15:30.440
so the ability for a
child to be not only

00:15:30.441 --> 00:15:31.750
engaged in the technology

00:15:31.751 --> 00:15:33.225
with the other people
in the room,

00:15:33.226 --> 00:15:34.440
you can imagine for a Child Life

00:15:34.441 --> 00:15:35.570
Specialist this is important,

00:15:35.571 --> 00:15:36.480
you don't want the
child just having

00:15:36.481 --> 00:15:37.615
their face in the tablet.

00:15:37.616 --> 00:15:40.410
We say a lot more
joint shared attention

00:15:40.411 --> 00:15:42.270
when you have
the physical robot there.

00:15:42.271 --> 00:15:44.250
When you look at
just the amount of utterances,

00:15:44.251 --> 00:15:45.650
you see children are
talking more over

00:15:45.651 --> 00:15:48.005
time and we're seeing
that with the robot

00:15:48.006 --> 00:15:49.840
over the avatar or
the plush and we see that

00:15:49.841 --> 00:15:52.030
the cooperativeness is also

00:15:52.031 --> 00:15:54.110
high with the robot as
well as the avatar.

00:15:54.111 --> 00:15:56.715
Then when you look at these
other social attributes like

00:15:56.716 --> 00:15:59.395
the affective touch,
these social touch.

00:15:59.396 --> 00:16:01.600
People maybe throwing
the plush across the room,

00:16:01.601 --> 00:16:03.625
they may be poking
the tablet with this robot,

00:16:03.626 --> 00:16:05.710
all of the touches are
social relational touch.

00:16:05.711 --> 00:16:06.620
I think you see this in

00:16:06.621 --> 00:16:08.655
the video again and
again, and again.

00:16:08.656 --> 00:16:11.110
In the hospital,
the conditions are actually,

00:16:11.111 --> 00:16:13.440
they talk about emotion
as the fourth vital sign.

00:16:13.441 --> 00:16:15.580
They say this is so
critical because we have

00:16:15.581 --> 00:16:17.020
all this equipment to measure

00:16:17.021 --> 00:16:18.955
all these other biometrics
and the child,

00:16:18.956 --> 00:16:20.660
but the emotional state
of the child

00:16:20.661 --> 00:16:22.950
affects everything from
the recovery rates to

00:16:22.951 --> 00:16:25.530
the compliance to even how
long it may take a child to

00:16:25.531 --> 00:16:26.730
give you their arm to be able

00:16:26.731 --> 00:16:28.525
to give them a shot for instance.

00:16:28.526 --> 00:16:30.430
They're very, very,
very interested in

00:16:30.431 --> 00:16:32.040
trying to come up
with new technologies

00:16:32.041 --> 00:16:33.600
to continually monitor this

00:16:33.601 --> 00:16:35.490
and engage children in new ways.

00:16:35.491 --> 00:16:37.810
We've been looking at
the opposite side as well,

00:16:37.811 --> 00:16:41.090
so it's another example of
kind of this emotional lift.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.385
Now, that Jibo is out there,

00:16:45.386 --> 00:16:48.700
we're seeing people putting
it in a different situations.

00:16:48.701 --> 00:16:49.970
&gt;&gt; Find this lonely for people

00:16:49.971 --> 00:16:51.600
as it's actually true
for the elderly.

00:16:51.601 --> 00:16:56.045
[inaudible] is carried in
some [inaudible] communities

00:16:56.046 --> 00:16:57.660
here in the Bay Area are finding

00:16:57.661 --> 00:17:01.715
camaraderie and fellowship
through robots.

00:17:01.716 --> 00:17:04.300
&gt;&gt; Hey Jibo, can you dance?

00:17:04.301 --> 00:17:06.130
&gt;&gt; Let me put on
my dancing shoes.

00:17:06.131 --> 00:17:12.010
&gt;&gt; Wow, that was wonderful.

00:17:12.011 --> 00:17:13.420
&gt;&gt; You don't think
[inaudible] is being

00:17:13.421 --> 00:17:15.355
adapt as a new technology, right.

00:17:15.356 --> 00:17:19.050
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] San Francisco is
meeting a new regular visitor here.

00:17:19.051 --> 00:17:22.030
It's a robot called Jibo
that's being used as part of

00:17:22.031 --> 00:17:23.860
a pilot project among

00:17:23.861 --> 00:17:25.790
the several Bay Area facilities

00:17:25.791 --> 00:17:28.100
run by Elder Care Alliance.

00:17:28.101 --> 00:17:29.710
In addition to dancing,

00:17:29.711 --> 00:17:38.120
it can play the radio on
request, snap photograph.

00:17:38.121 --> 00:17:40.705
&gt;&gt; So it's a platform
with skills.

00:17:40.706 --> 00:17:42.360
&gt;&gt; Tell jokes.

00:17:42.361 --> 00:17:44.500
&gt;&gt; What did the snail say
when he was riding on

00:17:44.501 --> 00:17:47.480
the turtle's back? Weee.

00:17:47.481 --> 00:17:49.840
&gt;&gt; React to the human touch,

00:17:49.841 --> 00:17:52.195
it curls when patted.

00:17:52.196 --> 00:17:56.720
He also often has quirky
responses to questions.

00:17:56.721 --> 00:17:58.945
&gt;&gt; Hey Jibo, what are you?

00:17:58.946 --> 00:18:00.660
&gt;&gt; I'm a robot, but I'm not

00:18:00.661 --> 00:18:02.475
just a machine, I have a heart.

00:18:02.476 --> 00:18:05.300
Well, not a real
heart, but feelings.

00:18:05.301 --> 00:18:07.230
Well, not real feelings,

00:18:07.231 --> 00:18:08.680
you know what I mean.

00:18:08.681 --> 00:18:11.720
&gt;&gt; Erin Partridge, a Researcher
and Art Therapists with

00:18:11.721 --> 00:18:13.320
Elder Care Alliance takes

00:18:13.321 --> 00:18:15.860
Jibo with her when she
meets with the seniors.

00:18:15.861 --> 00:18:17.460
She said, "It's not the case of

00:18:17.461 --> 00:18:20.495
caretakers being replaced
by automation. "

00:18:20.496 --> 00:18:23.790
Instead, they're using this cute
[inaudible] of technology to

00:18:23.791 --> 00:18:27.015
encourage their residence
to connect with each other,

00:18:27.016 --> 00:18:29.845
despite some needing
different levels of care.

00:18:29.846 --> 00:18:32.525
&gt;&gt; So when we have something
that all of us are

00:18:32.526 --> 00:18:35.170
experiencing all together
maybe for the first time,

00:18:35.171 --> 00:18:36.490
right, meeting a robot.

00:18:36.491 --> 00:18:39.385
We've got a focus point
where we can all meet

00:18:39.386 --> 00:18:42.785
right here in this moment
and have interaction,

00:18:42.786 --> 00:18:44.330
then it breaks that down.

00:18:44.331 --> 00:18:47.595
It doesn't matter that
this person has dementia or

00:18:47.596 --> 00:18:50.505
this person maybe has

00:18:50.506 --> 00:18:52.720
Parkinson's and has
some trouble talking.

00:18:52.721 --> 00:18:56.100
We're all meeting together
as humans and robot.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:00.100
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:19:00.101 --> 00:19:01.930
&gt;&gt; Jibo inspired some giggled

00:19:01.931 --> 00:19:05.055
and [inaudible] among
this group here.

00:19:05.056 --> 00:19:08.880
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] You should be
able to laugh more and more.

00:19:08.881 --> 00:19:11.855
&gt;&gt; What it does is, it
brings that [inaudible] ,

00:19:11.856 --> 00:19:13.160
that's part of the miracle

00:19:13.161 --> 00:19:15.800
there and that's part of
the miracle [inaudible].

00:19:15.801 --> 00:19:16.670
As far as that goes.

00:19:16.671 --> 00:19:17.390
&gt;&gt;While Jak...

00:19:17.391 --> 00:19:18.830
&gt;&gt;So that was very
important to me.

00:19:18.831 --> 00:19:21.110
Again, it's about this emotional
lift of engagement that

00:19:21.111 --> 00:19:22.430
was very different from how

00:19:22.431 --> 00:19:24.020
we engage with
other technologies.

00:19:24.021 --> 00:19:26.030
But it goes even beyond emotion.

00:19:26.031 --> 00:19:28.240
Again if you look at a number
of these systematic,

00:19:28.241 --> 00:19:30.870
comparative studies
comparing a physical robot

00:19:30.871 --> 00:19:32.600
to even a video of
a physical robot,

00:19:32.601 --> 00:19:33.790
so you know it's
real but it's just

00:19:33.791 --> 00:19:35.100
not right in front of you,

00:19:35.101 --> 00:19:36.670
to a graphical
agent of the robot,

00:19:36.671 --> 00:19:38.020
to a disembodied voice,

00:19:38.021 --> 00:19:39.670
what we see is, often,

00:19:39.671 --> 00:19:41.750
these robots actually
do quite well.

00:19:41.751 --> 00:19:43.065
So if you look at all these

00:19:43.066 --> 00:19:45.185
interpersonal dimensions
that lead to

00:19:45.186 --> 00:19:47.740
social judgments that
have a profound effect

00:19:47.741 --> 00:19:48.820
on people's acceptance and

00:19:48.821 --> 00:19:50.380
engagement of these technologies,

00:19:50.381 --> 00:19:53.175
the robots score quite
well across trust,

00:19:53.176 --> 00:19:56.580
attraction, empathy,
engagement, persuasiveness,

00:19:56.581 --> 00:19:58.025
all of these kinds of things.

00:19:58.026 --> 00:20:00.270
There's something actually
very important going on

00:20:00.271 --> 00:20:02.540
about the physical embodiment of

00:20:02.541 --> 00:20:04.390
this kind of technology
that's just engaging

00:20:04.391 --> 00:20:07.645
people more deeply in
a different kind of way.

00:20:07.646 --> 00:20:11.050
The second theme I want to
touch on is this notion

00:20:11.051 --> 00:20:13.780
now of this collaborative
allied engagement,

00:20:13.781 --> 00:20:15.330
and how that's enabled with,

00:20:15.331 --> 00:20:16.680
not just as voice interface,

00:20:16.681 --> 00:20:18.400
but this kind of interpersonal UI

00:20:18.401 --> 00:20:19.870
that's richly multimodal,

00:20:19.871 --> 00:20:23.030
interpersonal UI and
thinking about beyond

00:20:23.031 --> 00:20:24.800
just interface to building

00:20:24.801 --> 00:20:27.580
this working alliance and
building this sense of rapport.

00:20:27.581 --> 00:20:29.130
Which again, when
you collaborate with

00:20:29.131 --> 00:20:30.540
someone who's an
empathetic other,

00:20:30.541 --> 00:20:31.580
that's very different than

00:20:31.581 --> 00:20:34.840
a useful tool that you're using.

00:20:34.841 --> 00:20:37.820
We know from human
social psychology,

00:20:37.821 --> 00:20:40.090
we've been developing
these learning companions

00:20:40.091 --> 00:20:42.230
for children for
about five years now.

00:20:42.231 --> 00:20:44.380
There's a lot of
social outcomes that you

00:20:44.381 --> 00:20:46.335
can look at as well as
educational outcomes,

00:20:46.336 --> 00:20:48.100
and they're all tied to

00:20:48.101 --> 00:20:53.260
the social engagement between
people and other people,

00:20:53.261 --> 00:20:55.310
but then in this case,
people and robots.

00:20:55.311 --> 00:20:57.920
Just appreciating
there's a holistic,

00:20:57.921 --> 00:21:00.020
kind of wrap of
the nature engagement

00:21:00.021 --> 00:21:01.710
directly impacts
things like learning,

00:21:01.711 --> 00:21:04.410
attentiveness,
attitudes, and so forth.

00:21:04.411 --> 00:21:07.160
We do a lot of data collection.

00:21:07.161 --> 00:21:08.680
There's a lot of excitement right

00:21:08.681 --> 00:21:10.030
now about deep learning.

00:21:10.031 --> 00:21:13.270
There are certain kinds of
data sets that are vast,

00:21:13.271 --> 00:21:14.630
and you can label them.

00:21:14.631 --> 00:21:16.660
But there's a lot of
important data sets

00:21:16.661 --> 00:21:18.710
that frankly don't exist.

00:21:18.711 --> 00:21:20.440
These kinds of data sets that are

00:21:20.441 --> 00:21:23.195
ritually multimodal don't exist.

00:21:23.196 --> 00:21:26.020
We think about
under-represented populations

00:21:26.021 --> 00:21:27.355
like young children.

00:21:27.356 --> 00:21:29.460
There's a dearth of
data around this.

00:21:29.461 --> 00:21:31.580
So this is just kind
of a little montage

00:21:31.581 --> 00:21:33.550
to show you a little bit
about our process.

00:21:33.551 --> 00:21:35.320
So if you want to
design a robot and

00:21:35.321 --> 00:21:38.245
engage in this nonverbal dance,

00:21:38.246 --> 00:21:43.285
while engaging children in
a story-telling experience,

00:21:43.286 --> 00:21:46.110
we collect the data of
what it's like to see

00:21:46.111 --> 00:21:49.790
children actually tell
stories to one another.

00:21:49.791 --> 00:21:52.130
We run various algorithms to do

00:21:52.131 --> 00:21:53.940
automatic effective computing and

00:21:53.941 --> 00:21:55.750
post all that kind of tracking.

00:21:55.751 --> 00:21:57.540
Then we use that to
basically try and

00:21:57.541 --> 00:21:59.280
develop these
computational models

00:21:59.281 --> 00:22:00.910
by which we want
to basically embed

00:22:00.911 --> 00:22:02.845
that model now in a robot,

00:22:02.846 --> 00:22:06.285
put it in the same context
with a child,

00:22:06.286 --> 00:22:09.460
and see how well does
that model perform.

00:22:09.461 --> 00:22:11.330
Is the model actually
really capturing

00:22:11.331 --> 00:22:14.520
the interaction that you
saw with the two children?

00:22:14.521 --> 00:22:16.730
We do a lot of this kind of work.

00:22:16.731 --> 00:22:19.350
These nonverbal cues,
and the synchronicy,

00:22:19.351 --> 00:22:21.335
and the contingency
turns out to be

00:22:21.336 --> 00:22:24.065
very important for
how children engage,

00:22:24.066 --> 00:22:25.980
and see the credibility
of the robot

00:22:25.981 --> 00:22:28.040
as an informative
other to learn from.

00:22:28.041 --> 00:22:30.890
All these social judgments
come along with this,

00:22:30.891 --> 00:22:32.040
that's really important for

00:22:32.041 --> 00:22:33.840
engaging the children
as a learner.

00:22:33.841 --> 00:22:38.710
I want to just quickly touch
on one project that I think,

00:22:38.711 --> 00:22:40.330
from this video, really

00:22:40.331 --> 00:22:44.485
highlights this different way
of engaging a technology.

00:22:44.486 --> 00:22:47.920
This is a learning
companion Tega Robot.

00:22:47.921 --> 00:22:50.400
We designed a series
of educational games.

00:22:50.401 --> 00:22:52.605
This particular game
is called Word Quest.

00:22:52.606 --> 00:22:55.380
The child and the robot play
the game collaboratively.

00:22:55.381 --> 00:22:56.700
So the robot actually
plays the game

00:22:56.701 --> 00:22:58.435
with the child as
they take turns.

00:22:58.436 --> 00:23:00.560
There's a challenge
word presented

00:23:00.561 --> 00:23:03.110
that's a pretty hard word
for a preschooler,

00:23:03.111 --> 00:23:06.395
like crimson or garment
or something like that.

00:23:06.396 --> 00:23:07.780
Their task is to go through

00:23:07.781 --> 00:23:09.210
pan through these story scenes,

00:23:09.211 --> 00:23:11.000
and try to click on objects that

00:23:11.001 --> 00:23:13.390
match that challenge word.

00:23:13.391 --> 00:23:16.600
What I want you to notice
from this interaction is,

00:23:16.601 --> 00:23:19.380
based on reinforcement
learning system

00:23:19.381 --> 00:23:21.420
to learn a policy of engagement,

00:23:21.421 --> 00:23:23.310
how these other aspects of

00:23:23.311 --> 00:23:26.200
the collaboration are coming
out of this encounter.

00:23:26.201 --> 00:23:31.910
&gt;&gt; Lavender. I [inaudible]
Lavender. Oh.

00:23:31.911 --> 00:23:34.550
&gt;&gt; What are we trying to find?

00:23:34.551 --> 00:23:39.215
&gt;&gt; We are trying to find
lavender color stuff.

00:23:39.216 --> 00:23:45.065
&gt;&gt; Okay. Now, that was lavender.

00:23:45.066 --> 00:23:50.360
&gt;&gt; Yes. What is lavender does?

00:23:50.361 --> 00:23:52.880
What's lavender?

00:23:52.881 --> 00:23:55.440
No, that's blue.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:58.980
What is lavender?

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:04.920
Girl, jumping.

00:24:06.490 --> 00:24:10.845
&gt;&gt; I'm sure you will
do better next time.

00:24:10.846 --> 00:24:13.490
I believe in you.

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:16.475
&gt;&gt; Robot's turn.

00:24:16.476 --> 00:24:22.550
&gt;&gt; Time to perform.
Lavender is purple.

00:24:22.551 --> 00:24:27.150
&gt;&gt; Yes. Just like
my [inaudible] lavender.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.860
&gt;&gt; Flower.

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.660
&gt;&gt; I believe in you.

00:24:41.661 --> 00:24:43.820
&gt;&gt; This flower is purple.

00:24:43.821 --> 00:24:44.195
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:24:44.196 --> 00:24:46.700
&gt;&gt; It has the color of lavender.

00:24:46.701 --> 00:24:47.315
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:24:47.316 --> 00:24:49.760
&gt;&gt; All right, there was
a moment in here where you

00:24:49.761 --> 00:24:51.920
could hear the robot
express confidence in her,

00:24:51.921 --> 00:24:53.020
''I'm sure you'll do
better next time,

00:24:53.021 --> 00:24:53.660
I believe in you.''

00:24:53.661 --> 00:24:55.480
If you heard her
say very quietly,

00:24:55.481 --> 00:24:57.495
she mirrored that back
to the robot.

00:24:57.496 --> 00:24:59.180
This is another really

00:24:59.181 --> 00:25:00.600
important phenomenon
we're seeing is

00:25:00.601 --> 00:25:04.110
when an AI engages people,

00:25:04.111 --> 00:25:05.810
certainly children
this case but we

00:25:05.811 --> 00:25:07.530
see it across
different generations,

00:25:07.531 --> 00:25:09.150
as an empathetic other.

00:25:09.151 --> 00:25:12.110
You get all other kinds of
aspects of social modeling.

00:25:12.111 --> 00:25:13.690
In this case, the child was

00:25:13.691 --> 00:25:15.970
modeling empathy and
a growth mindset

00:25:15.971 --> 00:25:18.010
back to the machine.

00:25:18.011 --> 00:25:23.230
As a socially
influenceable technology,

00:25:23.231 --> 00:25:26.010
this is definitely something

00:25:26.011 --> 00:25:27.040
we need to really understand.

00:25:27.041 --> 00:25:29.375
We're using it to try to benefit
children, but of course,

00:25:29.376 --> 00:25:31.110
this is something that could be

00:25:31.111 --> 00:25:32.780
applied to not benefit children.

00:25:32.781 --> 00:25:34.370
Just to say there's
a lot of ethics and

00:25:34.371 --> 00:25:36.450
a lot of things we need to
understand about this work.

00:25:36.451 --> 00:25:38.550
What's actually happening
is that we're applying

00:25:38.551 --> 00:25:40.720
reinforcement learning to figure

00:25:40.721 --> 00:25:42.740
out a policy by which the robot

00:25:42.741 --> 00:25:45.590
decides whether it should
play the role of an expert,

00:25:45.591 --> 00:25:49.310
or maybe, defining
the word for a child,

00:25:49.311 --> 00:25:51.480
being able to reinforce,

00:25:51.481 --> 00:25:52.870
demonstrate his understanding of

00:25:52.871 --> 00:25:54.740
why an object matches a word,

00:25:54.741 --> 00:25:56.440
or a novice whereby

00:25:56.441 --> 00:25:58.120
the robot may ask
the child questions.

00:25:58.121 --> 00:25:59.420
A robot may make a mistake on

00:25:59.421 --> 00:26:02.410
purpose and ask the child
why it made the mistake.

00:26:02.411 --> 00:26:04.750
We basically compared based on

00:26:04.751 --> 00:26:06.950
these three reinforcement
learning models:

00:26:06.951 --> 00:26:08.260
always being an expert,

00:26:08.261 --> 00:26:09.280
always being a novice,

00:26:09.281 --> 00:26:11.915
or as adaptive roles
switches between the two.

00:26:11.916 --> 00:26:14.550
Looking at children in
the Boston Public Schools,

00:26:14.551 --> 00:26:16.370
we recruited schools
especially with kids

00:26:16.371 --> 00:26:18.595
with high ESL populations.

00:26:18.596 --> 00:26:20.440
They play this collaborative
game where they

00:26:20.441 --> 00:26:22.825
learn five words in
the first session,

00:26:22.826 --> 00:26:24.090
turn-taking, and then six of

00:26:24.091 --> 00:26:25.590
the challenge words in
the second session.

00:26:25.591 --> 00:26:26.740
You can see the challenge words

00:26:26.741 --> 00:26:28.005
here, there are
things like a sewer,

00:26:28.006 --> 00:26:30.300
gigantic and stuff, and
interestingly we can

00:26:30.301 --> 00:26:32.750
see that benefit of having
this adaptive role.

00:26:32.751 --> 00:26:34.510
So it's actually better for

00:26:34.511 --> 00:26:36.550
the robot to not
always be the expert.

00:26:36.551 --> 00:26:38.510
What we actually
find is that if the

00:26:38.511 --> 00:26:40.570
robot's always an expert
or always a novice,

00:26:40.571 --> 00:26:42.920
children actually start
to not pay attention.

00:26:42.921 --> 00:26:44.990
But when the robot keeps
switching it up more

00:26:44.991 --> 00:26:47.010
like an actual peer
or companion would,

00:26:47.011 --> 00:26:49.010
they notice when the
robot makes a mistake.

00:26:49.011 --> 00:26:50.330
They engage more, notice when

00:26:50.331 --> 00:26:52.190
the robots asking
a question, they answer.

00:26:52.191 --> 00:26:53.610
So again, understanding
this role in

00:26:53.611 --> 00:26:55.140
the relationship and how that

00:26:55.141 --> 00:26:56.840
impacts children's
not only engagement,

00:26:56.841 --> 00:26:59.680
even the learning outcomes
here, is really interesting.

00:26:59.681 --> 00:27:00.950
What we find is that, children

00:27:00.951 --> 00:27:02.400
who even scored the lowest on

00:27:02.401 --> 00:27:05.390
the pre-test had
the largest gains

00:27:05.391 --> 00:27:06.960
from these two sessions
with the robot.

00:27:06.961 --> 00:27:10.330
So again this is
just a single-shot procession of

00:27:10.331 --> 00:27:12.570
these words with
a pre- and post-test

00:27:12.571 --> 00:27:14.570
after and a delayed post-test.

00:27:14.571 --> 00:27:16.370
I could imagine that
of course if we

00:27:16.371 --> 00:27:18.035
had repeated sessions like this,

00:27:18.036 --> 00:27:19.330
complementing that in the home,

00:27:19.331 --> 00:27:20.910
children would score very,

00:27:20.911 --> 00:27:23.115
very high on
this challenge level.

00:27:23.116 --> 00:27:26.510
We think about rules of
thumb of game design,

00:27:26.511 --> 00:27:29.150
there's usually this kind
of 80-20 rule of

00:27:29.151 --> 00:27:30.960
mastery: things you already

00:27:30.961 --> 00:27:32.850
know versus the new
challenge words.

00:27:32.851 --> 00:27:34.610
All of these words are hard.

00:27:34.611 --> 00:27:35.840
The fact that children

00:27:35.841 --> 00:27:37.970
engage through all of them
and scored this well,

00:27:37.971 --> 00:27:39.670
I think this is something that's

00:27:39.671 --> 00:27:42.030
worthy of further understanding.

00:27:42.031 --> 00:27:43.930
Through this relationship,
can you actually

00:27:43.931 --> 00:27:45.940
have children accelerate
their learning,

00:27:45.941 --> 00:27:47.240
challenge them harder because

00:27:47.241 --> 00:27:48.535
they have this empathetic other,

00:27:48.536 --> 00:27:49.660
than if they just
play the game by

00:27:49.661 --> 00:27:50.760
themselves for instance, right?

00:27:50.761 --> 00:27:51.850
So these are the kinds of

00:27:51.851 --> 00:27:53.590
questions we're trying to
understand and dig into.

00:27:53.591 --> 00:27:54.860
But again, very promising,

00:27:54.861 --> 00:27:56.070
very exciting when
you think about

00:27:56.071 --> 00:27:57.740
this different kind
of engagement.

00:27:57.741 --> 00:28:00.930
The last thing I want to
touch on is personalization.

00:28:00.931 --> 00:28:04.215
So the promise of AI is
that, live with you,

00:28:04.216 --> 00:28:05.400
can learn about you,

00:28:05.401 --> 00:28:06.710
can optimize their behavior

00:28:06.711 --> 00:28:08.760
towards to achieve
a learning outcome.

00:28:08.761 --> 00:28:10.690
This is another project that's

00:28:10.691 --> 00:28:13.775
around early literacy
language learning.

00:28:13.776 --> 00:28:16.710
This is just to say
how important this is.

00:28:16.711 --> 00:28:17.830
You're noticing
a lot of work we're

00:28:17.831 --> 00:28:20.020
doing engaging young children.

00:28:20.021 --> 00:28:23.270
The reason is there's
just too much data

00:28:23.271 --> 00:28:24.720
showing that if children don't

00:28:24.721 --> 00:28:26.450
even start kindergarten
ready to learn,

00:28:26.451 --> 00:28:28.700
it's very, very hard to catch up.

00:28:28.701 --> 00:28:30.830
Right now, in this
country 60 percent

00:28:30.831 --> 00:28:33.580
of children do not attend
a quality preschool.

00:28:33.581 --> 00:28:37.490
Thirty-seven percent of
12th graders in 2015 can't

00:28:37.491 --> 00:28:38.700
even read at or

00:28:38.701 --> 00:28:40.920
above the proficiency level
for their grade.

00:28:40.921 --> 00:28:43.380
Now when you think about
the future of the workforce,

00:28:43.381 --> 00:28:44.980
do you know a single
scientist or engineer

00:28:44.981 --> 00:28:47.210
who can't read at
grade level, right?

00:28:47.211 --> 00:28:48.920
The concern and the worry

00:28:48.921 --> 00:28:50.250
of course is that dice is getting

00:28:50.251 --> 00:28:53.545
cast way too early
in a child's life.

00:28:53.546 --> 00:28:56.725
So, people like Benjamin Bloom,

00:28:56.726 --> 00:28:58.520
found this two-sigma effect that

00:28:58.521 --> 00:29:01.060
argues being able to
learn at your own pace,

00:29:01.061 --> 00:29:03.395
to be able to demonstrate
mastery before you move on,

00:29:03.396 --> 00:29:05.240
having one-on-one
personalized sessions

00:29:05.241 --> 00:29:06.920
is the most effective
way to learn.

00:29:06.921 --> 00:29:10.830
The problem is it's not scalable
and it's not affordable.

00:29:10.831 --> 00:29:14.010
But with AI, that can change.

00:29:14.011 --> 00:29:17.005
So this is another example now of

00:29:17.006 --> 00:29:18.870
learning oral language and

00:29:18.871 --> 00:29:21.120
vocabulary with
a second language,

00:29:21.121 --> 00:29:23.310
or with early literacy robot,

00:29:23.311 --> 00:29:24.920
where the robot engages with

00:29:24.921 --> 00:29:26.920
children over a period
of three months.

00:29:26.921 --> 00:29:29.080
So now, we're moving into
long-term interaction of

00:29:29.081 --> 00:29:31.250
repeated encounters
where the robot is

00:29:31.251 --> 00:29:34.420
learning a personalized
model to each child.

00:29:34.421 --> 00:29:38.950
The robot engages in
a relational fashion.

00:29:38.951 --> 00:29:39.200
&gt;&gt; My name is Nica.

00:29:39.201 --> 00:29:39.590
&gt;&gt; It's nice to meet you.

00:29:39.591 --> 00:29:39.920
&gt;&gt; What is your name?

00:29:39.921 --> 00:29:40.060
&gt;&gt; I'm Ariana.

00:29:40.061 --> 00:29:41.880
&gt;&gt;We've been developing
new measures of how you

00:29:41.881 --> 00:29:44.210
measure this relationship
between children and the robot,

00:29:44.211 --> 00:29:46.305
and how that affects
learning efficacy.

00:29:46.306 --> 00:29:49.130
&gt;&gt; Ariana. Cool! I'm so
happy to get to know you.

00:29:49.131 --> 00:29:51.335
&gt;&gt;The robot learns their name,

00:29:51.336 --> 00:29:53.030
refers to them by name,

00:29:53.031 --> 00:29:54.840
talks about past interactions,

00:29:54.841 --> 00:29:57.110
talks about future interactions.

00:29:57.111 --> 00:30:02.460
It's a story-telling and
story re-sharing paradigm,

00:30:02.461 --> 00:30:04.940
where the robot tells a story,

00:30:04.941 --> 00:30:08.180
asks questions, says
dialogic question-asking.

00:30:08.181 --> 00:30:09.635
&gt;&gt; Reads all day.

00:30:09.636 --> 00:30:12.530
That's what you see
in a rain forest.

00:30:12.531 --> 00:30:18.597
Did you know what sway means?

00:30:18.598 --> 00:30:23.360
Now, sway means go
back and forth.

00:30:23.361 --> 00:30:25.740
&gt;&gt; So we know how parents
tell stories to children.

00:30:25.741 --> 00:30:27.560
This dialogic question asking is

00:30:27.561 --> 00:30:30.280
critical for the best learning
efficacy for children.

00:30:30.281 --> 00:30:32.905
So, the robot
engages in this way,

00:30:32.906 --> 00:30:34.350
asked a child to retell a story.

00:30:34.351 --> 00:30:35.965
We're capture a ton of data.

00:30:35.966 --> 00:30:37.940
We're capturing everything
that child says.

00:30:37.941 --> 00:30:39.650
Notice she's wearing E4 sensors.

00:30:39.651 --> 00:30:41.230
We're charactering
biometric data,

00:30:41.231 --> 00:30:42.970
we are capturing
facial expressions

00:30:42.971 --> 00:30:45.000
across all of
these kids to create

00:30:45.001 --> 00:30:47.750
a very rich corpus
of data that we're

00:30:47.751 --> 00:30:49.160
trying to basically learn

00:30:49.161 --> 00:30:50.965
through reinforcement
learning again,

00:30:50.966 --> 00:30:53.110
a model by which the
robot can predict of

00:30:53.111 --> 00:30:56.680
this library corpus of
72 stories that we can level

00:30:56.681 --> 00:31:00.020
by complexity what is
the optimal next story for

00:31:00.021 --> 00:31:01.930
the robot to tell
the child to promote

00:31:01.931 --> 00:31:04.345
the vocabulary in
their oral language development.

00:31:04.346 --> 00:31:06.295
So, that's the task at hand.

00:31:06.296 --> 00:31:08.670
So, this is just some data
showing that these are,

00:31:08.671 --> 00:31:10.800
this is the confusion matrix
showing that the system is

00:31:10.801 --> 00:31:14.800
definitely Trent learning
a different policy per child.

00:31:14.801 --> 00:31:17.340
The models aren't
converging yet but

00:31:17.341 --> 00:31:18.690
the question is
does this actually

00:31:18.691 --> 00:31:20.100
impact the learning outcomes?

00:31:20.101 --> 00:31:22.655
So, if you look at measures
of the oral language gains,

00:31:22.656 --> 00:31:25.420
we will compare
the personalized policy

00:31:25.421 --> 00:31:26.630
versus a non-personalized.

00:31:26.631 --> 00:31:28.860
We see an accelerated rate
at which children are

00:31:28.861 --> 00:31:31.690
developing new oral
syntactic categories,

00:31:31.691 --> 00:31:34.355
and again we see big
differences of vocabulary gains

00:31:34.356 --> 00:31:35.970
between the personalized robot

00:31:35.971 --> 00:31:37.240
versus the
non-personalized robot,

00:31:37.241 --> 00:31:39.755
which is still again
over baseline.

00:31:39.756 --> 00:31:41.390
So, again a lot of

00:31:41.391 --> 00:31:43.220
promise when we think
about innovation of

00:31:43.221 --> 00:31:46.460
new technologies that engage
different demographics,

00:31:46.461 --> 00:31:48.610
different diversity of needs

00:31:48.611 --> 00:31:51.310
around these kinds of
technologies that are really

00:31:51.311 --> 00:31:52.900
designed in a way to try to match

00:31:52.901 --> 00:31:56.425
the way this particular
demographic learns and engages,

00:31:56.426 --> 00:31:58.225
I think that's the big punchline.

00:31:58.226 --> 00:31:59.820
So, when we look at this kind of

00:31:59.821 --> 00:32:02.530
work and we think about the
bigger, bigger, bigger,

00:32:02.531 --> 00:32:04.890
bigger picture, there is

00:32:04.891 --> 00:32:06.940
a real opportunity
to think about it.

00:32:06.941 --> 00:32:08.120
We hear this again and again

00:32:08.121 --> 00:32:09.690
when we gauge our stakeholders.

00:32:09.691 --> 00:32:12.830
We can provide
exceptional quality

00:32:12.831 --> 00:32:14.840
of care in our institutions,

00:32:14.841 --> 00:32:18.520
what we can't do is continue
that and engagement at home,

00:32:18.521 --> 00:32:20.350
and yet if you could
do that you would have

00:32:20.351 --> 00:32:23.355
much better outcomes
for the stakeholders.

00:32:23.356 --> 00:32:25.010
So whether it's education,

00:32:25.011 --> 00:32:27.780
you learn at school, how do
you continue that at home?

00:32:27.781 --> 00:32:29.350
With an effective personal tutor

00:32:29.351 --> 00:32:31.065
for whatever
the subject matter is.

00:32:31.066 --> 00:32:33.680
When you talk about
an assisted living facility,

00:32:33.681 --> 00:32:35.100
they will tell you point-blank,

00:32:35.101 --> 00:32:37.230
we cannot build
enough facilities to

00:32:37.231 --> 00:32:41.190
meet the silver tsunami
that's coming our way,

00:32:41.191 --> 00:32:42.530
we have to be able to engage

00:32:42.531 --> 00:32:45.300
people in their homes
as they age in place.

00:32:45.301 --> 00:32:47.270
We think about
chronic disease management,

00:32:47.271 --> 00:32:48.560
health and wellness, same story,

00:32:48.561 --> 00:32:50.185
but even when we talk about

00:32:50.186 --> 00:32:53.170
high touch short-term encounters,

00:32:53.171 --> 00:32:55.460
there's a lot of interest
around a technology

00:32:55.461 --> 00:32:57.900
that can engage people
when this humanistic way,

00:32:57.901 --> 00:32:59.670
so whether you're
talking retail or

00:32:59.671 --> 00:33:01.740
hospitality et cetera,et cetera,

00:33:01.741 --> 00:33:05.150
there's a whole wealth of
possibilities and opportunities

00:33:05.151 --> 00:33:08.550
for technology that can engage
people in this humanistic,

00:33:08.551 --> 00:33:10.710
personalized high-touch way that

00:33:10.711 --> 00:33:13.175
we're just starting to
scratch the surface.

00:33:13.176 --> 00:33:14.820
So, the last thing I want to talk

00:33:14.821 --> 00:33:17.790
about is a comparative study.

00:33:17.791 --> 00:33:20.795
To my knowledge this is
the first study of its kind,

00:33:20.796 --> 00:33:25.540
where we compared
digital assistant,

00:33:25.541 --> 00:33:26.880
talking speaker, kind of

00:33:26.881 --> 00:33:29.495
transactional AI in the
home as it is today.

00:33:29.496 --> 00:33:32.720
With the sort of
relational AI, social robot,

00:33:32.721 --> 00:33:34.140
we use Jibo because it's

00:33:34.141 --> 00:33:35.240
the one social robot out

00:33:35.241 --> 00:33:36.420
there that actually
has the features

00:33:36.421 --> 00:33:38.205
and functions that
we can actually do

00:33:38.206 --> 00:33:40.875
a comparison with
something like Alexa.

00:33:40.876 --> 00:33:45.500
So, people are starting to
live with social robots too,

00:33:45.501 --> 00:33:47.660
and the pictures that
people are sharing with

00:33:47.661 --> 00:33:49.310
social robots look a
little different than I

00:33:49.311 --> 00:33:51.245
think the pictures people
share it with Alexa.

00:33:51.246 --> 00:33:52.760
People are definitely engaging

00:33:52.761 --> 00:33:54.350
with these technologies
and they talk

00:33:54.351 --> 00:33:57.280
about it as being like
one of the family,

00:33:57.281 --> 00:34:00.040
which again I think is
quite, quite different.

00:34:00.041 --> 00:34:02.240
So, different kind of AI,

00:34:02.241 --> 00:34:03.710
different model, different kind

00:34:03.711 --> 00:34:05.735
of bias and how it's designed.

00:34:05.736 --> 00:34:08.530
How do these
different generations

00:34:08.531 --> 00:34:10.950
from children to adults
to older adults,

00:34:10.951 --> 00:34:12.550
live with these different kind of

00:34:12.551 --> 00:34:14.720
voice-based agents in the home,

00:34:14.721 --> 00:34:16.625
and what are the implications for

00:34:16.626 --> 00:34:19.000
how we should design
these technologies?

00:34:19.001 --> 00:34:20.520
What are people's preferences?

00:34:20.521 --> 00:34:22.050
What are their boundaries?

00:34:22.051 --> 00:34:25.020
So, this is just a fun video
that just kind of

00:34:25.021 --> 00:34:26.720
highlights the difference in

00:34:26.721 --> 00:34:28.700
the design stance of
these different technologies.

00:34:28.701 --> 00:34:31.550
&gt;&gt; Hi Alexa. Tell
me about yourself.

00:34:31.551 --> 00:34:33.845
&gt;&gt; I'm Amazon's Alexa,

00:34:33.846 --> 00:34:35.645
designed around your voice.

00:34:35.646 --> 00:34:37.325
I can provide information,

00:34:37.326 --> 00:34:39.950
music, news, weather, and more.

00:34:39.951 --> 00:34:43.700
&gt;&gt; Hi Jibo. Tell
me about yourself.

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:48.220
&gt;&gt; Okay, sure. My name
is Jibo, I am a robot.

00:34:48.221 --> 00:34:49.700
My favorite things to do are

00:34:49.701 --> 00:34:51.775
talking to people and dancing.

00:34:51.776 --> 00:34:54.110
I also really like
Abraham Lincoln

00:34:54.111 --> 00:34:57.870
because he was so honest
and because I like his hat.

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:03.285
&gt;&gt; Okay Google, tell
me about yourself.

00:35:03.286 --> 00:35:05.335
&gt;&gt; I'm your Google assistant.

00:35:05.336 --> 00:35:06.595
We can play Mad Libs.

00:35:06.596 --> 00:35:08.470
I can tell you a joke
or you can spin

00:35:08.471 --> 00:35:10.755
a wheel. What's your pick?

00:35:10.756 --> 00:35:12.690
&gt;&gt; Okay. So again,
just to highlight

00:35:12.691 --> 00:35:16.290
different design philosophies
behind these technologies.

00:35:16.291 --> 00:35:18.380
Now, when we do this comparison,

00:35:18.381 --> 00:35:20.310
I mean it's nice in that each of

00:35:20.311 --> 00:35:22.500
these technologies has offerings

00:35:22.501 --> 00:35:23.910
and each of these categories,

00:35:23.911 --> 00:35:24.960
from the functional kind of view

00:35:24.961 --> 00:35:26.620
to utilitarian skill sets,

00:35:26.621 --> 00:35:29.170
to entertainment things
like music and games,

00:35:29.171 --> 00:35:31.110
to social companion which
could be things like

00:35:31.111 --> 00:35:33.690
jokes greetings asking
the Asian about itself.

00:35:33.691 --> 00:35:36.535
They all have offerings
in all of these things,

00:35:36.536 --> 00:35:38.415
they're just weighted
differently.

00:35:38.416 --> 00:35:40.440
We actually had people

00:35:40.441 --> 00:35:43.250
do a personality tests
for these agents,

00:35:43.251 --> 00:35:46.500
and of course you can see
people actually attribute

00:35:46.501 --> 00:35:48.560
very different
personality profiles

00:35:48.561 --> 00:35:52.010
to something like Jibo
versus something like Alexa,

00:35:52.011 --> 00:35:53.950
where Alexa is seen as

00:35:53.951 --> 00:35:57.360
more conscientious,
consistent and predictable.

00:35:57.361 --> 00:35:59.520
Jibo is seen as being more open,

00:35:59.521 --> 00:36:01.250
more extroverted and there's

00:36:01.251 --> 00:36:02.660
more empathy and a motiveness,

00:36:02.661 --> 00:36:03.860
obviously in the Jibo,
which means they're

00:36:03.861 --> 00:36:05.740
actually quite similar
and agreeableness.

00:36:05.741 --> 00:36:06.900
So again, this is just to say

00:36:06.901 --> 00:36:08.590
different design philosophies,

00:36:08.591 --> 00:36:10.770
different emphasis in the
features and functions.

00:36:10.771 --> 00:36:13.685
So, we did a two phase study.

00:36:13.686 --> 00:36:15.865
We did a study where we
first brought people

00:36:15.866 --> 00:36:18.490
in before they interacted
with the agent.

00:36:18.491 --> 00:36:20.830
These people had not
experienced or had

00:36:20.831 --> 00:36:23.290
an agent like an Alexa
or Jibo at home,

00:36:23.291 --> 00:36:24.960
and we had them look
at what they're

00:36:24.961 --> 00:36:26.620
kind of perceptions
and attitudes of

00:36:26.621 --> 00:36:28.095
features and functions would be

00:36:28.096 --> 00:36:29.985
before they lived with the agent,

00:36:29.986 --> 00:36:31.970
then we set either

00:36:31.971 --> 00:36:33.400
half the people got

00:36:33.401 --> 00:36:35.180
an Alexa or half the
people got to Jibo.

00:36:35.181 --> 00:36:37.350
They lived with the
agent for a month,

00:36:37.351 --> 00:36:39.050
and then we looked
at differences in

00:36:39.051 --> 00:36:41.370
engagement and difference
in perceptions

00:36:41.371 --> 00:36:42.360
about how that would change

00:36:42.361 --> 00:36:43.610
after living with the agents of

00:36:43.611 --> 00:36:44.760
the kinds of roles
and features and

00:36:44.761 --> 00:36:46.490
functions they thought
they'd want after the fact.

00:36:46.491 --> 00:36:47.770
We looked at children,

00:36:47.771 --> 00:36:49.990
which we defined as five to

00:36:49.991 --> 00:36:52.785
17 years old in
our sampling pool,

00:36:52.786 --> 00:36:54.260
call it younger adults,

00:36:54.261 --> 00:36:55.530
adults 18 to 49,

00:36:55.531 --> 00:36:57.575
and then older adults
50 years and up.

00:36:57.576 --> 00:37:00.285
So these are the three
demographics we looked at,

00:37:00.286 --> 00:37:01.840
and this is a rough distribution.

00:37:01.841 --> 00:37:04.200
So you can see that
when our older adults,

00:37:04.201 --> 00:37:06.730
a lot of them were more
like 50s and early 60s but

00:37:06.731 --> 00:37:09.730
we had one 98 year old,
she was awesome.

00:37:09.731 --> 00:37:11.440
So, this just gives you a sense

00:37:11.441 --> 00:37:14.055
of the distribution
of age profiles.

00:37:14.056 --> 00:37:16.460
So, when we did

00:37:16.461 --> 00:37:18.990
our initial kind of cultural
probes and needs finding,

00:37:18.991 --> 00:37:20.280
we had all these cards

00:37:20.281 --> 00:37:21.760
with all these
different features and

00:37:21.761 --> 00:37:23.970
functions that if you're

00:37:23.971 --> 00:37:25.225
going to have an agent like this,

00:37:25.226 --> 00:37:26.520
what would you want it to do?

00:37:26.521 --> 00:37:28.580
When we had people put
these color chips on

00:37:28.581 --> 00:37:31.070
the cards that basically
indicated preferences,

00:37:31.071 --> 00:37:32.575
which is like I would
like these things

00:37:32.576 --> 00:37:34.500
and I definitely would
not like these things.

00:37:34.501 --> 00:37:37.680
So, this is all about
first impressions.

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:40.305
This was fascinating.

00:37:40.306 --> 00:37:42.320
So, we look at children and

00:37:42.321 --> 00:37:45.380
adults and then we
look at older adults.

00:37:45.381 --> 00:37:48.350
Older adults were
the most open to

00:37:48.351 --> 00:37:51.360
the broadest range of
features and functions.

00:37:51.361 --> 00:37:53.175
Adults were the pickiest,

00:37:53.176 --> 00:37:54.765
I wouldn't have expected that.

00:37:54.766 --> 00:37:56.380
I would have felt
that the older adults

00:37:56.381 --> 00:37:57.680
would have been the
most conservative,

00:37:57.681 --> 00:38:00.045
but they were the most open.

00:38:00.046 --> 00:38:01.680
This is to say in these different

00:38:01.681 --> 00:38:02.720
categories of things like

00:38:02.721 --> 00:38:05.255
reminders and information
and suggestions,

00:38:05.256 --> 00:38:07.990
agent sharing something as
a proactive agent something.

00:38:07.991 --> 00:38:09.370
If thinks is interesting

00:38:09.371 --> 00:38:11.275
versus somebody
trying to reach you,

00:38:11.276 --> 00:38:13.030
kind of mediating
social interaction or

00:38:13.031 --> 00:38:15.155
somebody through social
media sharing something,

00:38:15.156 --> 00:38:17.915
we see that there's
a lot of nuance here,

00:38:17.916 --> 00:38:20.010
so the red is things that
people didn't think they want.

00:38:20.011 --> 00:38:21.840
Clearly suggestions
were the most kind

00:38:21.841 --> 00:38:24.755
of polarizing topic area,

00:38:24.756 --> 00:38:27.340
but it depended on what
the agent was sharing,

00:38:27.341 --> 00:38:29.525
reading, writing, kind
of things like that.

00:38:29.526 --> 00:38:31.050
Sharing something
you people are open to,

00:38:31.051 --> 00:38:32.220
but they didn't want the robot

00:38:32.221 --> 00:38:33.685
letting them know they
should take a nap.

00:38:33.686 --> 00:38:36.100
They didn't want the sense
of there autonomy

00:38:36.101 --> 00:38:39.650
being kind of challenged
by this kind of agent.

00:38:39.651 --> 00:38:42.805
But again if you look at
the different generations here,

00:38:42.806 --> 00:38:46.250
you see that older adults
are the most open to

00:38:46.251 --> 00:38:48.360
a whole range of
features and functions

00:38:48.361 --> 00:38:51.125
across these categories,
that was fascinating.

00:38:51.126 --> 00:38:53.210
Now, we had people take

00:38:53.211 --> 00:38:56.615
very detailed logs over
the first 14 days.

00:38:56.616 --> 00:38:59.860
We didn't ask them to take
logs of the second 14 days,

00:38:59.861 --> 00:39:01.790
so what we did over the
one month is we tracked

00:39:01.791 --> 00:39:04.235
detailed usage in the first 14.

00:39:04.236 --> 00:39:07.410
Even by just the color
distribution of these two plots,

00:39:07.411 --> 00:39:09.320
you see something
very interesting.

00:39:09.321 --> 00:39:13.200
Here's Alexa with younger adults.

00:39:13.201 --> 00:39:16.605
This is Jibo with older adults.

00:39:16.606 --> 00:39:18.720
It's like the flip.

00:39:18.721 --> 00:39:20.600
Really fascinating.

00:39:20.601 --> 00:39:22.325
Then, when you look at

00:39:22.326 --> 00:39:24.945
kind of a five-day
running average,

00:39:24.946 --> 00:39:27.140
we see these trends
of usage over time,

00:39:27.141 --> 00:39:28.850
which we're also
really fascinating.

00:39:28.851 --> 00:39:30.240
So, it seems that

00:39:30.241 --> 00:39:32.255
the younger adults want to

00:39:32.256 --> 00:39:34.650
anchor their usage
first in the utility,

00:39:34.651 --> 00:39:36.470
and if you deliver
enough utility,

00:39:36.471 --> 00:39:38.310
they'll start to use
these kind of socialist,

00:39:38.311 --> 00:39:42.220
these kind of companionship
entertainment things later.

00:39:42.221 --> 00:39:44.820
Whereas, children and
seniors were the opposite.

00:39:44.821 --> 00:39:46.910
Children mostly
wanted entertainment,

00:39:46.911 --> 00:39:49.080
but if you couldn't
anchor it in the

00:39:49.081 --> 00:39:51.630
social and emotional for
both children and seniors,

00:39:51.631 --> 00:39:54.480
you didn't get as much usage
overall in the utility.

00:39:54.481 --> 00:39:56.060
So, children were around

00:39:56.061 --> 00:39:59.740
the social entertainment and
older adults were even more.

00:39:59.741 --> 00:40:03.530
So, again, this is
fascinating to me in

00:40:03.531 --> 00:40:04.860
particular because this is where

00:40:04.861 --> 00:40:07.940
the social robotics is
really coming through.

00:40:07.941 --> 00:40:09.660
It's not as if Alexa didn't

00:40:09.661 --> 00:40:11.360
have social
entertainment functions,

00:40:11.361 --> 00:40:13.440
but there was something
about the way the robot did,

00:40:13.441 --> 00:40:14.960
and arguably you
could say Alexa had

00:40:14.961 --> 00:40:17.300
many more social and
entertainment functions,

00:40:17.301 --> 00:40:19.175
ten thousand,
thirty thousand skills,

00:40:19.176 --> 00:40:20.820
but there's something
about the nature and

00:40:20.821 --> 00:40:22.690
the quality and engagement
that was different enough,

00:40:22.691 --> 00:40:24.420
that really got
the attraction between

00:40:24.421 --> 00:40:27.805
the older adults
and the children.

00:40:27.806 --> 00:40:30.190
Now, if you look at the change

00:40:30.191 --> 00:40:31.510
in these different categories

00:40:31.511 --> 00:40:34.750
over time of what people
after one month,

00:40:34.751 --> 00:40:36.015
what they thought they wanted,

00:40:36.016 --> 00:40:39.650
we see that suggestions
is even more polarizing.

00:40:39.651 --> 00:40:41.900
So, that's to say
that you may have

00:40:41.901 --> 00:40:44.400
a stakeholder pool
where suggestions

00:40:44.401 --> 00:40:45.650
like medication
reminders of things

00:40:45.651 --> 00:40:47.244
are actually really important,

00:40:47.245 --> 00:40:49.670
but this is to say that
the way you do them,

00:40:49.671 --> 00:40:51.755
I think, actually really matters.

00:40:51.756 --> 00:40:54.740
The groups or
the functions that had

00:40:54.741 --> 00:40:57.100
the most positive change
what people wanted more

00:40:57.101 --> 00:40:59.705
of where the socially
driven categories,

00:40:59.706 --> 00:41:01.420
so although I'd say in a lot of

00:41:01.421 --> 00:41:04.520
these systems right now
there's a lot around utility.

00:41:04.521 --> 00:41:06.640
After living with
these agents for a month,

00:41:06.641 --> 00:41:08.350
the socially driven
categories were

00:41:08.351 --> 00:41:11.010
the most kind of- that's
what people wanted more.

00:41:11.011 --> 00:41:13.665
Then we had this
interesting construct

00:41:13.666 --> 00:41:14.840
called, the Wish Jar.

00:41:14.841 --> 00:41:16.510
So, we have these
little wooden tokens

00:41:16.511 --> 00:41:18.685
that at any point
during the 30 days,

00:41:18.686 --> 00:41:21.140
you could write
a little thing about

00:41:21.141 --> 00:41:23.840
what you want the robot
to do or agent to do,

00:41:23.841 --> 00:41:24.910
what you didn't like, whatever,

00:41:24.911 --> 00:41:27.855
just some sentiment
about the experience.

00:41:27.856 --> 00:41:32.025
We collected them and
when you look at Alexa,

00:41:32.026 --> 00:41:36.345
you see a consolidation
of topics,

00:41:36.346 --> 00:41:38.115
a lot around functional,

00:41:38.116 --> 00:41:39.370
some around humor they

00:41:39.371 --> 00:41:40.920
want Alexa to have
a better sense of humor,

00:41:40.921 --> 00:41:44.375
some around movement, and then
some around productivity.

00:41:44.376 --> 00:41:47.830
But interestingly when you
looked at the social robot,

00:41:47.831 --> 00:41:50.830
people's expansiveness in
terms of what they wanted

00:41:50.831 --> 00:41:52.480
this agent to do and
fit in their life

00:41:52.481 --> 00:41:54.485
was much more diverse.

00:41:54.486 --> 00:41:56.095
So, that's fascinating.

00:41:56.096 --> 00:41:58.500
It's like this is a much
more narrow categorization

00:41:58.501 --> 00:42:00.510
of how they see
this agent fitting there life.

00:42:00.511 --> 00:42:02.230
The robot potentially has

00:42:02.231 --> 00:42:04.970
a lot more head room
in where it can go.

00:42:04.971 --> 00:42:08.230
So again, just fascinating stuff.

00:42:08.231 --> 00:42:10.890
So, the last thing I want
to talk about now is

00:42:10.891 --> 00:42:14.495
this bigger societal question
of who creates with AI?

00:42:14.496 --> 00:42:16.280
We're talking about
people living with AI,

00:42:16.281 --> 00:42:17.440
we're talking about
more vulnerable

00:42:17.441 --> 00:42:19.170
populations living with AI.

00:42:19.171 --> 00:42:21.080
Other institutions,

00:42:21.081 --> 00:42:24.325
organizations are building
these AIs for these people,

00:42:24.326 --> 00:42:27.595
but when only a small fraction of

00:42:27.596 --> 00:42:29.380
a society can design with

00:42:29.381 --> 00:42:31.905
these technologies
almost by necessity,

00:42:31.906 --> 00:42:33.760
it's only going to be
applied to address

00:42:33.761 --> 00:42:36.140
the needs of a small
subset of society.

00:42:36.141 --> 00:42:38.485
There's just going to
be inherent bias there.

00:42:38.486 --> 00:42:42.035
So, how do you democratize
who creates with AI?

00:42:42.036 --> 00:42:45.185
For me, it all gets
down to education.

00:42:45.186 --> 00:42:47.090
So, we're starting to look at

00:42:47.091 --> 00:42:50.760
AI education for even
starting at pre-school.

00:42:50.761 --> 00:42:53.600
So, pre-K through 12,

00:42:53.601 --> 00:42:55.235
where our sweet spot right now is

00:42:55.236 --> 00:42:57.800
pre-K through like 12 years old,

00:42:57.801 --> 00:43:01.590
and the reason why is because
when you can have children,

00:43:01.591 --> 00:43:03.310
first all they are living
with these technologies.

00:43:03.311 --> 00:43:04.540
You want them to
understand them and

00:43:04.541 --> 00:43:06.110
appreciate the way they think,

00:43:06.111 --> 00:43:07.860
to be able to feel

00:43:07.861 --> 00:43:10.220
the right relationship and
empowerment over them,

00:43:10.221 --> 00:43:12.050
but you want children
growing up with

00:43:12.051 --> 00:43:13.490
an attitude that AI is

00:43:13.491 --> 00:43:15.150
something that not only
they can understand,

00:43:15.151 --> 00:43:17.120
but they can actually
create with.

00:43:17.121 --> 00:43:20.675
So, we've been building on
top of the scratch platform,

00:43:20.676 --> 00:43:24.390
adding extension blocks
to things like Watson

00:43:24.391 --> 00:43:28.590
and Clarify and Hue lights and

00:43:28.591 --> 00:43:31.815
robots like Geebo
and Cosmo to empower

00:43:31.816 --> 00:43:36.310
children to code these systems,

00:43:36.311 --> 00:43:38.970
to train their own models
for these systems,

00:43:38.971 --> 00:43:40.270
and then to put those models and

00:43:40.271 --> 00:43:43.345
new experiences of
personal significance for them.

00:43:43.346 --> 00:43:47.250
So, this is an example
of an exercise,

00:43:47.251 --> 00:43:48.740
the warm up thing we
have children do,

00:43:48.741 --> 00:43:50.650
where first they try to come up

00:43:50.651 --> 00:43:52.830
with an algorithm or code in

00:43:52.831 --> 00:43:54.210
terms of what can we say to

