a third-party and not
the person themselves, right?

00:54:46.751 --> 00:54:48.630
So it's just to say, we live in

00:54:48.631 --> 00:54:51.975
an interesting times
but we are seeing

00:54:51.976 --> 00:54:58.545
a very provocative psychology
happening here.

00:54:58.546 --> 00:55:03.050
Again, it runs deep and I think
the more we do this work,

00:55:03.051 --> 00:55:05.920
we're discovering how
deep it actually goes.

00:55:07.910 --> 00:55:11.100
&gt;&gt; A lot of the agents have

00:55:11.101 --> 00:55:13.590
this novelty effect like
that the kids see it,

00:55:13.591 --> 00:55:15.224
they get super excited,

00:55:15.225 --> 00:55:18.675
and it is hard to
make it permanent

00:55:18.676 --> 00:55:22.140
like build this longer-term
friendship with them.

00:55:22.141 --> 00:55:25.635
What are your thoughts about
this continued engagement?

00:55:25.636 --> 00:55:27.030
How you can actually form

00:55:27.031 --> 00:55:29.265
bonds and go beyond the novelty?

00:55:29.266 --> 00:55:30.720
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, so I mean I think

00:55:30.721 --> 00:55:32.260
you can see in a number
of these studies they're

00:55:32.261 --> 00:55:34.050
longitudinal so
they're going well

00:55:34.051 --> 00:55:37.000
beyond the kind of
novelty effect.

00:55:37.001 --> 00:55:39.340
I actually think in
our field we need to

00:55:39.341 --> 00:55:41.730
have a real deep systematic
investigation of what

00:55:41.731 --> 00:55:44.110
we mean by the novelty
effect because

00:55:44.111 --> 00:55:46.505
when I was first
a graduate student,

00:55:46.506 --> 00:55:48.840
it was like a five-minute
encounter before

00:55:48.841 --> 00:55:49.980
you started the task was enough

00:55:49.981 --> 00:55:51.475
to address the novelty effect.

00:55:51.476 --> 00:55:52.870
Now, it's like two weeks and you

00:55:52.871 --> 00:55:54.210
haven't addressed
the novelty effect,

00:55:54.211 --> 00:55:56.080
so we just need to be
much more rigorous and

00:55:56.081 --> 00:55:58.375
what we mean when we
say the novelty effect.

00:55:58.376 --> 00:56:01.540
That said, when you're
interacting with the system over

00:56:01.541 --> 00:56:03.490
three months or a month every day

00:56:03.491 --> 00:56:05.685
in the home you passed
the novelty effect.

00:56:05.686 --> 00:56:07.740
So, it's a combination of things.

00:56:07.741 --> 00:56:12.030
I do think there is
definitely you need

00:56:12.031 --> 00:56:15.130
enough freshness
in the activities

00:56:15.131 --> 00:56:16.460
or it's like no matter how

00:56:16.461 --> 00:56:18.180
compelling Angry Birds is
it's going to get boring,

00:56:18.181 --> 00:56:20.410
so you need enough variability
and freshness of

00:56:20.411 --> 00:56:23.055
the activities to sustain
any sort of encounter.

00:56:23.056 --> 00:56:24.950
I think these relational things

00:56:24.951 --> 00:56:26.230
are also really important.

00:56:26.231 --> 00:56:29.770
So, the fact that the robot
whether it's Jibo or Tega are

00:56:29.771 --> 00:56:33.900
actually doing these
social grooming functions.

00:56:33.901 --> 00:56:35.040
Greeting you in the morning,

00:56:35.041 --> 00:56:36.220
asking you how your day was,

00:56:36.221 --> 00:56:38.995
remembering that,
personalizing that to you,

00:56:38.996 --> 00:56:41.810
commenting on did you have
another good night's sleep or

00:56:41.811 --> 00:56:44.700
I'm sorry I hope you sleep
better tomorrow night,

00:56:44.701 --> 00:56:45.880
did you sleep better tonight.

00:56:45.881 --> 00:56:46.940
I mean these things

00:56:46.941 --> 00:56:48.760
matter and I guess that's
what we're finding is

00:56:48.761 --> 00:56:52.920
the personalization
combined I think with the,

00:56:52.921 --> 00:56:54.870
again the supportive empathetic

00:56:54.871 --> 00:56:58.630
other actually matters to people.

00:56:58.631 --> 00:57:00.210
So, I think it's a combination of

00:57:00.211 --> 00:57:02.310
those things and there's
other things as well.

00:57:02.311 --> 00:57:06.580
But, we're at the very beginning

00:57:06.581 --> 00:57:07.980
of understanding how you design

00:57:07.981 --> 00:57:10.690
for sustained relationship and

00:57:10.691 --> 00:57:13.550
you're starting to see
mechanisms were exploring here.

00:57:13.551 --> 00:57:15.220
It's hard to do this work,

00:57:15.221 --> 00:57:17.680
it's hard to deploy
these systems these robot

00:57:17.681 --> 00:57:18.940
systems in people's homes for

00:57:18.941 --> 00:57:20.240
a long period of time
to collect the data,

00:57:20.241 --> 00:57:22.900
it's just hard, it's
logistically hard and expensive

00:57:22.901 --> 00:57:26.390
to do but it's important work
that needs to be done.

00:57:26.391 --> 00:57:29.630
So, it's just to say
there's a lot of

00:57:29.631 --> 00:57:33.409
opportunity here I think
and very poignant.

00:57:33.410 --> 00:57:35.940
But just like
interpersonal interaction,

00:57:35.941 --> 00:57:39.690
I think there's
a whole set of processes

00:57:39.691 --> 00:57:42.170
that are at play to build
that sustained sense

00:57:42.171 --> 00:57:43.710
of not only just engagement,

00:57:43.711 --> 00:57:45.470
but that kind of relationship

00:57:45.471 --> 00:57:46.180
that you feel like you're

00:57:46.181 --> 00:57:47.350
actually working with the other,

00:57:47.351 --> 00:57:48.820
or for sustained period
of time to reach

00:57:48.821 --> 00:57:51.060
a goal of personal significance.

00:57:51.061 --> 00:57:53.680
I think that's also part of it.

00:57:56.110 --> 00:57:58.450
&gt;&gt; I thought your part

00:57:58.451 --> 00:58:02.060
about the AITutor was
really, really interesting.

00:58:02.061 --> 00:58:06.150
What are some of
the next steps that you see

00:58:06.151 --> 00:58:09.065
applications for the AITutor

00:58:09.066 --> 00:58:11.255
given the technology
that we have right now?

00:58:11.256 --> 00:58:13.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I mean, obviously

00:58:13.026 --> 00:58:15.220
computer tutors is a huge field.

00:58:15.221 --> 00:58:17.460
I think it tends
to be applied more

00:58:17.461 --> 00:58:19.800
towards older students
and seems to

00:58:19.801 --> 00:58:22.290
be tentative quite
more towards like

00:58:22.291 --> 00:58:25.895
math and physics and kind of
STEM, explicit STEM areas.

00:58:25.896 --> 00:58:29.530
We were going after the younger,

00:58:29.531 --> 00:58:31.610
early childhood learning
for a number of reasons.

00:58:31.611 --> 00:58:33.350
One is just it's
a critical time to intervene.

00:58:33.351 --> 00:58:35.320
As I talked about, it's

00:58:35.321 --> 00:58:39.265
an underexplored area for
technological innovation.

00:58:39.266 --> 00:58:41.920
Children at that age just
learn in a very different way.

00:58:41.921 --> 00:58:44.850
They absolutely learn from
friendly, empathetic others.

00:58:44.851 --> 00:58:45.930
That's how they're
wired to learn,

00:58:45.931 --> 00:58:48.125
so you need to design
to support that.

00:58:48.126 --> 00:58:50.980
I think there's a whole host
of things we need to

00:58:50.981 --> 00:58:53.400
address around early
childhood learning,

00:58:53.401 --> 00:58:55.230
around early math and literacy.

00:58:55.231 --> 00:58:57.950
Even the fastest growing
demographic internet

00:58:57.951 --> 00:59:00.220
public schools are
English language learners,

00:59:00.221 --> 00:59:02.110
which is why we are going
out to recruit from

00:59:02.111 --> 00:59:04.645
schools with high English
language learning populations.

00:59:04.646 --> 00:59:08.320
Kids with special needs is
another huge area, right?

00:59:08.321 --> 00:59:10.930
So, whether it's
attentional issues,

00:59:10.931 --> 00:59:12.935
or ASD, or whatnot,

00:59:12.936 --> 00:59:15.140
I think there's
huge opportunities

00:59:15.141 --> 00:59:17.070
for again kind of giving

00:59:17.071 --> 00:59:19.230
everyone the best
possible chance of

00:59:19.231 --> 00:59:21.845
being successful by addressing
a diversity of learners.

00:59:21.846 --> 00:59:26.875
That's where I think a lot of
high impact opportunity is.

00:59:26.876 --> 00:59:28.280
On the flip side, I think you can

00:59:28.281 --> 00:59:29.540
look at workforce retraining.

00:59:29.541 --> 00:59:30.875
With all that kind of

00:59:30.876 --> 00:59:33.130
concern about AI
replacing our jobs,

00:59:33.131 --> 00:59:34.700
I think there's
a huge opportunity for AI

00:59:34.701 --> 00:59:36.855
to actually train
us for new jobs.

00:59:36.856 --> 00:59:38.925
So, I think that
could become a new,

00:59:38.926 --> 00:59:42.850
another kind of huge
impact area for education.

00:59:42.851 --> 00:59:44.920
There's a lot more
discussion around kind of

00:59:44.921 --> 00:59:47.755
lifelong learning as well
and continuing education.

00:59:47.756 --> 00:59:49.820
So, it's all important.

00:59:49.821 --> 00:59:52.730
But for me, just after
looking at all of

00:59:52.731 --> 00:59:56.470
the data and all the
unmet opportunity,

00:59:56.471 --> 00:59:58.580
it's the early childhood stuff.

00:59:58.581 --> 01:00:00.670
The fact that the die is being

01:00:00.671 --> 01:00:02.845
cast for way too many children,

01:00:02.846 --> 01:00:05.905
way too early in their
life. It's just not right.

01:00:05.906 --> 01:00:08.020
So, I do think that's going
to be the biggest kind

01:00:08.021 --> 01:00:09.770
of social justice area of

01:00:09.771 --> 01:00:15.155
intervention for
education. You asked when?

01:00:15.156 --> 01:00:17.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [inaudible].
At one point,

01:00:17.471 --> 01:00:19.870
you were showing
how people perceive

01:00:19.871 --> 01:00:22.795
the personalities
of Alexa and Jibo.

01:00:22.796 --> 01:00:25.460
I'm wondering for Jibo,
how did that match

01:00:25.461 --> 01:00:26.770
the intended design of

01:00:26.771 --> 01:00:27.850
Jibo's personality or is there

01:00:27.851 --> 01:00:29.525
anything that
surprised you there?

01:00:29.526 --> 01:00:31.850
&gt;&gt; I think that's-
that did match I think

01:00:31.851 --> 01:00:34.220
the kind of design stance
of how Jibo was designed.

01:00:34.221 --> 01:00:37.755
I mean, he's designed to be
open and extroverted and to,

01:00:37.756 --> 01:00:39.280
you know, convey and elicit

01:00:39.281 --> 01:00:41.500
this emotive kind of
empathetic response.

01:00:41.501 --> 01:00:42.990
We would like it if he was

01:00:42.991 --> 01:00:45.495
perceived to be
more conscientious.

01:00:45.496 --> 01:00:47.940
You know, [inaudible] technology

01:00:47.941 --> 01:00:49.920
continues to improve
and evolve you know,

01:00:49.921 --> 01:00:51.665
but- and he just doesn't,

01:00:51.666 --> 01:00:53.735
he doesn't have
10 thousand skills chat.

01:00:53.736 --> 01:00:55.905
So, there's some of
that at play as well.

01:00:55.906 --> 01:00:57.550
But in terms of, you know,

01:00:57.551 --> 01:01:00.190
I think again
the punchline for me,

01:01:00.191 --> 01:01:03.440
especially that working
average of seeing the trends,

01:01:03.441 --> 01:01:06.100
is to say that's where
the social robotics

01:01:06.101 --> 01:01:08.830
is really showing
up because Alexa

01:01:08.831 --> 01:01:12.040
has arguably more
offerings in all of

01:01:12.041 --> 01:01:13.275
those categories

01:01:13.276 --> 01:01:14.680
than a product like
Jibo that's only been

01:01:14.681 --> 01:01:17.450
on the market
a few months and yet,

01:01:17.451 --> 01:01:18.900
you see this different
in engagement.

01:01:18.901 --> 01:01:20.710
That's the social robotics
coming through.

01:01:20.711 --> 01:01:22.850
That's a different way that
the robot are showing up to

01:01:22.851 --> 01:01:25.535
people in their lives that's
leading to that engagement.

01:01:25.536 --> 01:01:27.100
I think this is
something the field

01:01:27.101 --> 01:01:29.335
or even on the
consumer landscape,

01:01:29.336 --> 01:01:33.230
any corporations
have yet to really

01:01:33.231 --> 01:01:36.920
grasp is that this is
actually, this is different.

01:01:36.921 --> 01:01:39.705
This kind of experience
is quite different.

01:01:39.706 --> 01:01:42.910
And you know, it's appealing to

01:01:42.911 --> 01:01:45.000
a diversity of users

01:01:45.001 --> 01:01:47.240
that I think it's also
noteworthy, right?

01:01:47.241 --> 01:01:50.140
So, I think the punchline
for me is also,

01:01:50.141 --> 01:01:52.400
if younger adults
are are keying in on

01:01:52.401 --> 01:01:54.910
utility that's just a matter
of time for social robots,

01:01:54.911 --> 01:01:56.720
they're going to
have the skills too.

01:01:56.721 --> 01:01:58.080
So, at some point,

01:01:58.081 --> 01:01:59.250
that's going to be mood and then,

01:01:59.251 --> 01:02:02.280
it really is about
these other aspects.

01:02:02.281 --> 01:02:05.080
It is about this other
relational AI aspects.

01:02:05.081 --> 01:02:08.240
And that technology to me
that says in the long run,

01:02:08.241 --> 01:02:11.040
social robotics are going to
be the next thing, right?

01:02:11.041 --> 01:02:12.090
Because you can't just always

01:02:12.091 --> 01:02:14.510
be disembodied talking
speakers, right?

01:02:14.511 --> 01:02:16.355
Clearly that's going
to be the glide path

01:02:16.356 --> 01:02:18.600
and we're starting to see
it in this engagement data.

01:02:18.601 --> 01:02:26.045
And again, it just gets down
to the way our brains work.

01:02:26.046 --> 01:02:29.760
We evolve to want to connect and

01:02:29.761 --> 01:02:33.565
relate to animate
entities in this way,

01:02:33.566 --> 01:02:36.500
and you know, the more you
design to support that,

01:02:36.501 --> 01:02:38.610
the richer the engagement, right?

01:02:38.611 --> 01:02:40.140
So, I think it's not

01:02:40.141 --> 01:02:41.780
surprising when you
think about it that way,

01:02:41.781 --> 01:02:44.830
but I think the industry
has to kind of catch up.

01:02:44.831 --> 01:02:45.995
I think right now,

01:02:45.996 --> 01:02:47.250
our spoken language systems are

01:02:47.251 --> 01:02:48.830
very much like playing chess.

01:02:48.831 --> 01:02:50.535
It's like discrete turns,

01:02:50.536 --> 01:02:53.360
but communication, human
communication is it's a dance.

01:02:53.361 --> 01:02:55.025
It's not playing chess.

01:02:55.026 --> 01:02:56.620
It's mutually regulated.

01:02:56.621 --> 01:02:57.750
It's dynamic. It's fluid.

01:02:57.751 --> 01:02:58.940
It's all of these things

01:02:58.941 --> 01:03:00.350
and that's where
all these other things

01:03:00.351 --> 01:03:04.260
like liking and trust and
affiliation come to play.

01:03:04.261 --> 01:03:06.920
So.

01:03:06.921 --> 01:03:07.880
&gt;&gt; Give another chance.

01:03:07.881 --> 01:03:09.185
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:03:09.186 --> 01:03:11.660
&gt;&gt; I'm just, maybe this is

01:03:11.661 --> 01:03:13.370
projecting way too
far in the future,

01:03:13.371 --> 01:03:15.250
but I remember reading an article

01:03:15.251 --> 01:03:18.090
where it was said that dogs

01:03:18.091 --> 01:03:20.320
have been bred to
the point where they

01:03:20.321 --> 01:03:23.380
prefer the company of humans
to the company of dogs.

01:03:23.381 --> 01:03:25.500
And so, it's not that

01:03:25.501 --> 01:03:28.510
far-stretched to think that
eventually the social robot

01:03:28.511 --> 01:03:32.080
will be socially far
superior to other humans and

01:03:32.081 --> 01:03:33.820
then humans may start

01:03:33.821 --> 01:03:35.440
preferring the company of robots

01:03:35.441 --> 01:03:36.965
to the company of humans,

01:03:36.966 --> 01:03:40.350
and how far in the future
do we think that is?

01:03:40.351 --> 01:03:40.665
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:03:40.666 --> 01:03:43.065
&gt;&gt; We have already
observed, how this is?

01:03:43.066 --> 01:03:47.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I will say
this, people need people.

01:03:47.221 --> 01:03:51.050
We need to feel that we
belong to our community.

01:03:51.051 --> 01:03:53.940
We need to feel valued
by our community,

01:03:53.941 --> 01:03:55.430
that our community
cares about us.

01:03:55.431 --> 01:03:59.495
We can't flourish if we
don't have that and so,

01:03:59.496 --> 01:04:00.990
I think these robots and

01:04:00.991 --> 01:04:05.355
these intelligent technologies
are going to be,

01:04:05.356 --> 01:04:09.450
a wonderful augmentation
of society,

01:04:09.451 --> 01:04:13.980
but people still will
always need people.

01:04:13.981 --> 01:04:15.880
And these technologies should be

01:04:15.881 --> 01:04:18.515
designed to enhance
and support that.

01:04:18.516 --> 01:04:21.365
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure I
get your premise.

01:04:21.366 --> 01:04:24.605
I mean, it's certainly
not true for dogs.

01:04:24.606 --> 01:04:27.450
&gt;&gt; Well, so- but I'm not
talking about the dog.

01:04:27.451 --> 01:04:29.520
I'm talking about what
it is to be human.

01:04:29.521 --> 01:04:31.080
Humans need humans.

01:04:31.081 --> 01:04:32.815
I'm not saying dogs can't be

01:04:32.816 --> 01:04:34.710
bred to prefer humans over dogs.

01:04:34.711 --> 01:04:37.900
I'm saying the way human
beings are by our very nature,

01:04:37.901 --> 01:04:40.420
we are profoundly
social species, right?

01:04:40.421 --> 01:04:42.345
We've evolved to need.

01:04:42.346 --> 01:04:44.470
You know, social pain
is now being

01:04:44.471 --> 01:04:47.220
appreciated to be
real pain, right?

01:04:47.221 --> 01:04:49.310
It's not just this kind
of stiff upper lip thing,

01:04:49.311 --> 01:04:51.040
it's processed in
the same regions

01:04:51.041 --> 01:04:52.360
of the brain. It's actual pain.

01:04:52.361 --> 01:04:53.410
We're just, you know,

01:04:53.411 --> 01:04:55.020
chronic loneliness is a series of

01:04:55.021 --> 01:04:56.370
a health condition as smoking

01:04:56.371 --> 01:04:57.480
and obesity and substance abuse.

01:04:57.481 --> 01:04:58.650
It's like we're just
starting to really

01:04:58.651 --> 01:04:59.990
appreciate the neuroscience,

01:04:59.991 --> 01:05:02.025
of how deeply social we are.

01:05:02.026 --> 01:05:04.845
So, people need people.

01:05:04.846 --> 01:05:06.740
&gt;&gt; You're making the premise of

01:05:06.741 --> 01:05:08.710
what you're saying is
based on the fact that we

01:05:08.711 --> 01:05:10.795
will never have more empathy

01:05:10.796 --> 01:05:13.470
for robot than for
human, is that correct?

01:05:13.471 --> 01:05:15.870
&gt;&gt; I think that
human relationships

01:05:15.871 --> 01:05:17.810
are complex and messy and

01:05:17.811 --> 01:05:22.200
important and they make
life worth living. I do.

01:05:22.201 --> 01:05:24.440
There's something about
the human-human relationship

01:05:24.441 --> 01:05:25.430
that I don't think
you're going to be able

01:05:25.431 --> 01:05:26.730
to replace with a machine.

01:05:26.731 --> 01:05:27.930
I don't think you can't

01:05:27.931 --> 01:05:29.070
create machines that
are going to help

01:05:29.071 --> 01:05:31.180
benefit people in
profoundly important ways,

01:05:31.181 --> 01:05:33.440
but I don't think
they're going to replace

01:05:33.441 --> 01:05:36.670
what's important about
human connection to people.

01:05:36.671 --> 01:05:39.120
And I'd be willing to say I think

01:05:39.121 --> 01:05:41.520
the neuroscience is
going to show that.

01:05:41.521 --> 01:05:44.200
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] question.
[inaudible] to define

01:05:44.201 --> 01:05:47.720
social robotics as robots
with social skills?

01:05:47.721 --> 01:05:50.330
&gt;&gt; So, I think like

01:05:50.331 --> 01:05:52.540
all aspects of robots
you can probably

01:05:52.541 --> 01:05:54.350
put a panel of experts on

01:05:54.351 --> 01:05:55.630
social robotics and
probably give you

01:05:55.631 --> 01:05:57.155
different definitions of
what a social robot is,

01:05:57.156 --> 01:05:58.680
just like they're going
to give you definition,

01:05:58.681 --> 01:06:00.260
different definitions
of what a robot is.

01:06:00.261 --> 01:06:02.495
So, they absolutely
have social skills.

01:06:02.496 --> 01:06:04.250
I would say it's
beyond that though.

01:06:04.251 --> 01:06:08.980
They are engaging...it's how
they engage people, right?

01:06:08.981 --> 01:06:12.685
They are engaging
these social thinking,

01:06:12.686 --> 01:06:16.115
reasoning mechanisms of a person.

01:06:16.116 --> 01:06:19.845
&gt;&gt; Would it be fair that
they operate in a way,

01:06:19.846 --> 01:06:22.200
in a form of social engineering?

01:06:23.090 --> 01:06:25.240
&gt;&gt; I don't know. I
don't understand.

01:06:25.241 --> 01:06:27.450
Form of social engineering.
I mean, there...

01:06:27.451 --> 01:06:28.245
&gt;&gt; Manipulation.

01:06:28.246 --> 01:06:30.280
Through the simulation of

01:06:30.281 --> 01:06:32.800
social or human
like social skills.

01:06:32.801 --> 01:06:34.490
&gt;&gt; So I would say
that absolutely.

01:06:34.491 --> 01:06:36.350
So, as they are designed,

01:06:36.351 --> 01:06:38.430
the human mind engages

01:06:38.431 --> 01:06:42.205
with them in a way
that we evolved to be,

01:06:42.206 --> 01:06:43.760
and you can design

01:06:43.761 --> 01:06:46.390
these systems to use
that to benefit people.

01:06:46.391 --> 01:06:48.290
&gt;&gt; And I guess that
Patricia's comment was

01:06:48.291 --> 01:06:50.820
in for as there's

01:06:50.821 --> 01:06:55.615
the danger of
manipulating robusticity,

01:06:55.616 --> 01:06:58.460
so the question is, there

01:06:58.461 --> 01:07:00.695
was this video about
the, what was it,

01:07:00.696 --> 01:07:07.720
the ledger about this ledger
that Google released so far.

01:07:07.721 --> 01:07:10.520
Who is motivating
this social robots

01:07:10.521 --> 01:07:14.495
to that we're going to have?

01:07:14.496 --> 01:07:17.755
&gt;&gt; This is what's so
important to discuss.

01:07:17.756 --> 01:07:21.680
Yeah. So, whether it's virtual
reality and people's [inaudible]

01:07:21.681 --> 01:07:23.790
sensors being stimulated
like it's the real thing

01:07:23.791 --> 01:07:26.350
or whether it's
advertising or cinema,

01:07:26.351 --> 01:07:30.635
we're not strangers to artifacts
manipulating us, right?

01:07:30.636 --> 01:07:32.675
We do this all the time

01:07:32.676 --> 01:07:34.240
in many, many
different capacities.

01:07:34.241 --> 01:07:36.075
Our games are advertised,

01:07:36.076 --> 01:07:38.660
this is not
the new new thing it's

01:07:38.661 --> 01:07:41.210
just to say that it can
happen here as well

01:07:41.211 --> 01:07:42.610
and we need to understand it

01:07:42.611 --> 01:07:44.280
and we need to have
dialogues around

01:07:44.281 --> 01:07:46.930
best practices and how

01:07:46.931 --> 01:07:48.430
it should be implied
to benefit people.

01:07:48.431 --> 01:07:50.740
Because clearly,
the good news here

01:07:50.741 --> 01:07:53.765
is that it can really
benefit people,

01:07:53.766 --> 01:07:57.040
but you don't want
unintended consequences, right?

01:07:57.041 --> 01:07:58.650
&gt;&gt; I love you're saying
this. Because this is

01:07:58.651 --> 01:08:02.350
a very perhaps
subtle atomic bomb.

01:08:02.351 --> 01:08:05.470
So you saying, let's have
children interact with

01:08:05.471 --> 01:08:09.455
robots that are experts
in social engineering.

01:08:09.456 --> 01:08:12.400
Go. So, having that

01:08:12.401 --> 01:08:14.700
running without concern of

01:08:14.701 --> 01:08:16.290
people like you
will be dangerous.

01:08:16.291 --> 01:08:18.280
&gt;&gt; And I would say it's
like anytime you're

01:08:18.281 --> 01:08:20.565
trying any form of
learning right.

01:08:20.566 --> 01:08:23.675
Designing a serious game, right?

01:08:23.676 --> 01:08:25.475
I mean, this is,

01:08:25.476 --> 01:08:28.810
what this is is a different
dimension of designing

01:08:28.811 --> 01:08:30.830
technologies that interface with

01:08:30.831 --> 01:08:32.300
the human mind and
behavior, right?

01:08:32.301 --> 01:08:35.755
That's what all of this is
essentially about right?

01:08:35.756 --> 01:08:38.960
And is to say, just like too
much screen time right now

01:08:38.961 --> 01:08:40.520
people are willing to
say that screens can

01:08:40.521 --> 01:08:42.605
be addictive to kids, right?

01:08:42.606 --> 01:08:44.480
It's to say eyes wide open with

01:08:44.481 --> 01:08:46.430
any technology with
any age group,

01:08:46.431 --> 01:08:49.155
I wouldn't say just kids,
with any age group.

01:08:49.156 --> 01:08:52.145
Tremendous benefit as possible

01:08:52.146 --> 01:08:55.205
but you got to keep
eyes wide open.

01:08:55.206 --> 01:09:00.785
Absolutely. So, you wanted
to ask the question.

01:09:00.786 --> 01:09:03.880
&gt;&gt; The charts, how

01:09:03.881 --> 01:09:07.490
did you measure and
benchmark emphathy.

01:09:07.491 --> 01:09:08.835
&gt;&gt; On?

01:09:08.836 --> 01:09:10.280
&gt;&gt; There were like
multiple charts

01:09:10.281 --> 01:09:11.630
and empathy was showing.

01:09:11.631 --> 01:09:13.475
&gt;&gt; So, this was literally like

01:09:13.476 --> 01:09:15.330
a personality test
that you would give to

01:09:15.331 --> 01:09:17.730
people that people filled
out for the agent.

01:09:17.731 --> 01:09:20.570
So, there was like
standard questions asked on

01:09:20.571 --> 01:09:24.090
a personality test is what
this what that represented.

01:09:24.091 --> 01:09:25.220
&gt;&gt; So, the people measured

01:09:25.221 --> 01:09:26.560
empirically based on
their understanding-

01:09:26.561 --> 01:09:28.080
&gt;&gt; So, people were
asked a question,

01:09:28.081 --> 01:09:28.720
like here's a set of

01:09:28.721 --> 01:09:29.950
questions how would
you rate the agent.

01:09:29.951 --> 01:09:30.870
Just like if you are taking

01:09:30.871 --> 01:09:32.470
a personality test for yourself,

01:09:32.471 --> 01:09:33.740
fill it out for yourself,

01:09:33.741 --> 01:09:35.570
they were doing
that for the agent.

01:09:35.571 --> 01:09:37.570
Here's a set of questions,
how would you fill it out?

01:09:37.571 --> 01:09:39.110
So that's what that
was generated from.

01:09:39.111 --> 01:09:41.480
It's literally like a personality
test, but just applied.

01:09:41.481 --> 01:09:43.740
People applied it to the agent.

01:09:44.170 --> 01:09:47.450
&gt;&gt; Curious. Have
you noticed maybe

01:09:47.451 --> 01:09:50.675
an analogy with
the uncanny valley

01:09:50.676 --> 01:09:53.330
with social interactions
like if people kind of

01:09:53.331 --> 01:09:54.520
recoiled like that's too

01:09:54.521 --> 01:09:57.075
realistic or kind of a weird out.

01:09:57.076 --> 01:09:59.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah so the uncanny valley
I think it's something

01:09:59.761 --> 01:10:03.220
that people throw
that term out a lot.

01:10:03.221 --> 01:10:05.540
Really what it means is the

01:10:05.541 --> 01:10:09.015
more human-like
something becomes,

01:10:09.016 --> 01:10:13.720
you go into this kind of
feels creepy kind of factor.

01:10:13.721 --> 01:10:17.045
So I think for me
a more recent example is

01:10:17.046 --> 01:10:21.555
human animated
characters in film.

01:10:21.556 --> 01:10:24.905
At the time of
the first tin toy Pixar,

01:10:24.906 --> 01:10:25.840
I don't know if you
remember the baby

01:10:25.841 --> 01:10:27.085
in a concrete diaper.

01:10:27.086 --> 01:10:28.695
Way in the valley,

01:10:28.696 --> 01:10:29.960
way in the valley,

01:10:29.961 --> 01:10:32.515
but then at some point Shrek,

01:10:32.516 --> 01:10:34.425
whatever, clearly we could

01:10:34.426 --> 01:10:36.040
tell that there
wasn't a real human,

01:10:36.041 --> 01:10:38.230
but the aesthetic was
out of the valley right?

01:10:38.231 --> 01:10:40.200
So something as abstract as

01:10:40.201 --> 01:10:41.950
Geebo isn't really something

01:10:41.951 --> 01:10:43.180
that's going to
fall in the valley,

01:10:43.181 --> 01:10:47.580
because it's meant to be how
exactly human-like it looks,

01:10:47.581 --> 01:10:50.720
it's kind of that conjecture
is really about.

01:10:50.721 --> 01:10:54.820
But, I would say that when
you ask people creepy,

01:10:54.821 --> 01:10:57.230
creepy can mean any number
of things to people,

01:10:57.231 --> 01:11:00.005
it could mean creepy because
it seems so life-like,

01:11:00.006 --> 01:11:03.460
to another person would make
it seem really cool right?

01:11:03.461 --> 01:11:04.680
And then there's kind of creepy

01:11:04.681 --> 01:11:05.840
like walking corpse creepy,

01:11:05.841 --> 01:11:07.540
which is that's what
the uncanny valley

01:11:07.541 --> 01:11:09.170
I think was really getting on.

01:11:09.171 --> 01:11:12.365
&gt;&gt; One more, I just
have a quick question.

01:11:12.366 --> 01:11:15.360
When do you think Geebo
will get arms and legs?

01:11:15.361 --> 01:11:19.460
like we have all these Alexas

01:11:19.461 --> 01:11:20.790
and things like speakers that are

01:11:20.791 --> 01:11:22.880
stationary but when
are you suppose.

01:11:22.881 --> 01:11:24.230
&gt;&gt; So, I would say you know when

01:11:24.231 --> 01:11:26.140
people ask me what
a social robot is,

01:11:26.141 --> 01:11:27.580
a social robot is not

01:11:27.581 --> 01:11:30.090
something that only sits
on your countertop.

01:11:30.091 --> 01:11:31.980
I happened to build those systems

01:11:31.981 --> 01:11:33.900
because I want to
focus on the social.

01:11:33.901 --> 01:11:36.685
But I would argue Baxter
is a social robot,

01:11:36.686 --> 01:11:38.770
because it was designed to
work collaboratively with

01:11:38.771 --> 01:11:40.120
the person and it's
got the screen

01:11:40.121 --> 01:11:41.635
with the eyes and the attention.

01:11:41.636 --> 01:11:43.945
It's a social
manufacturing robot,

01:11:43.946 --> 01:11:45.910
so you know Toyota research,

01:11:45.911 --> 01:11:47.110
other people are going to create

01:11:47.111 --> 01:11:49.720
mobile manipulator social robots

01:11:49.721 --> 01:11:50.800
because they have to live with

01:11:50.801 --> 01:11:52.970
people and it's that social
interpersonal competence

01:11:52.971 --> 01:11:54.640
that's going to be critical for

01:11:54.641 --> 01:11:56.720
fitting into people's lives
and them wanting to have it

01:11:56.721 --> 01:11:59.330
around and for actually being
collaborative and helpful.

01:11:59.331 --> 01:12:02.125
So, I just think it's all,

01:12:02.126 --> 01:12:04.255
it's all going to happen.

01:12:04.256 --> 01:12:06.950
Okay can you [inaudible].

01:12:06.951 --> 01:12:08.930
&gt;&gt; I thought it was
interesting that

01:12:08.931 --> 01:12:11.420
the interaction
was sort of better

01:12:11.421 --> 01:12:13.730
when the robot switched from
being expert to novice,

01:12:13.731 --> 01:12:15.965
because I think
a lot of us do like

01:12:15.966 --> 01:12:17.120
robot as a student or

01:12:17.121 --> 01:12:19.115
robot as a teacher,
but sort of doing.

01:12:19.116 --> 01:12:21.260
But how did you decide what

01:12:21.261 --> 01:12:23.555
exactly an expert and
novice meant because,

01:12:23.556 --> 01:12:25.970
I think the way we interact
with each other there's

01:12:25.971 --> 01:12:27.530
like a graded scale of

01:12:27.531 --> 01:12:29.165
how we're an expert
and how we're novice.

01:12:29.166 --> 01:12:30.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, there were

01:12:30.531 --> 01:12:33.280
specifically different
behaviors that

01:12:33.281 --> 01:12:35.800
the robot would do
in either role.

01:12:35.801 --> 01:12:37.070
So, in novice role,

01:12:37.071 --> 01:12:39.710
the robot may ask a question and

01:12:39.711 --> 01:12:41.250
the reason why it's really
asking the question

01:12:41.251 --> 01:12:42.845
it's because it wants
the child to articulate,

01:12:42.846 --> 01:12:44.040
because that's going
to help them learn,

01:12:44.041 --> 01:12:46.170
but it's playing the
role of the novice.

01:12:46.171 --> 01:12:48.720
Or in the novice role the
robot would intentionally make

01:12:48.721 --> 01:12:50.170
a mistake and ask

01:12:50.171 --> 01:12:52.300
the child why do you
think that was wrong.

01:12:52.301 --> 01:12:55.095
So those are examples
of novice behaviors.

01:12:55.096 --> 01:12:57.050
Expert behaviors would be things

01:12:57.051 --> 01:12:58.650
like giving the definition of

01:12:58.651 --> 01:13:03.140
the word or giving the
child a hint, right?

01:13:03.141 --> 01:13:05.640
So in this particular
implementation,

01:13:05.641 --> 01:13:07.700
there were just different
kinds of behaviors the robot

01:13:07.701 --> 01:13:10.400
would do in one
role or the other.

01:13:10.401 --> 01:13:12.820
We use reinforcement
learning plus

01:13:12.821 --> 01:13:17.620
some adaptation to switch
the roles dynamically

01:13:17.621 --> 01:13:21.780
based on how the child
was doing both from

01:13:21.781 --> 01:13:23.920
a kind of affective
computing standpoint as

01:13:23.921 --> 01:13:26.285
well as their actual performance
in the game, right?

01:13:26.286 --> 01:13:30.370
So the robot was always
making decisions based on

01:13:30.371 --> 01:13:32.430
how do I best support
this child in

01:13:32.431 --> 01:13:34.480
this moment even as it

01:13:34.481 --> 01:13:36.770
was kind of taking
this different kind of role.

01:13:36.771 --> 01:13:38.370
But what we definitely
saw anecdotally

01:13:38.371 --> 01:13:40.670
was children kind of,

01:13:40.671 --> 01:13:42.470
they're kind of they came a

01:13:42.471 --> 01:13:44.569
little more of
a lean back experience

01:13:44.570 --> 01:13:46.260
when the robot's behavior
was predictable

01:13:46.261 --> 01:13:48.275
and it always kind of took
one role or the other,

01:13:48.276 --> 01:13:50.000
whereas when it was mixing it up,

01:13:50.001 --> 01:13:52.910
they noticed more, and
what we also found,

01:13:52.911 --> 01:13:54.455
again the social modeling thing,

01:13:54.456 --> 01:13:57.690
is the more the robot talks
about its own process,

01:13:57.691 --> 01:14:01.025
the more children talk out
loud about their process.

01:14:01.026 --> 01:14:02.740
So, we see the social modeling

01:14:02.741 --> 01:14:03.990
happening again and again in

01:14:03.991 --> 01:14:06.820
so many different constructs
and in this case

01:14:06.821 --> 01:14:08.150
it's obviously done in

01:14:08.151 --> 01:14:09.810
a way to promote
the children's learning,

01:14:09.811 --> 01:14:11.170
because the more
they articulate and

01:14:11.171 --> 01:14:13.350
reflect and verbalize the

01:14:13.351 --> 01:14:14.830
more likely they are
to actually learn

01:14:14.831 --> 01:14:17.435
these things, right? So.

01:14:17.436 --> 01:14:21.320
&gt;&gt; In each role is the robot
learning what just- say,

01:14:21.321 --> 01:14:23.070
because I can imagine like
in the future and it has

01:14:23.071 --> 01:14:25.620
some sort of part of behavior.

01:14:25.621 --> 01:14:31.305
&gt;&gt; So it's not learning on
the fly how to generate,

01:14:31.306 --> 01:14:33.015
there's a set of kinds of

01:14:33.016 --> 01:14:35.635
parameterized utterances
it's going to say,

01:14:35.636 --> 01:14:37.705
so it's kind of more
templatized of these form.

01:14:37.706 --> 01:14:39.040
In the future, yeah,

01:14:39.041 --> 01:14:42.310
you can imagine having that
be much more flexible.

01:14:42.311 --> 01:14:44.350
But in the case of
this particular work it was

01:14:44.351 --> 01:14:48.025
more templated kinds
of responses.

01:14:48.026 --> 01:14:50.300
Okay. Good?

01:14:50.301 --> 01:14:50.930
&gt;&gt; Thank you again Cynthia.

01:14:50.931 --> 01:14:52.500
&gt;&gt; Great. Thank you

